// WHY: ps3t.6.7 slice 5a — the ERROR-RECOVERY differential ORACLE.
//
// A corpus of INVALID programs, each asserted to recover IDENTICALLY under two
// independent implementations of the same grammar. Because typeck gates on parse
// errors (no codegen runs for an invalid program), the diagnostics ARE the
// observable output, so this is where recovery behaviour is pinned at all.
//
// ── WHICH TWO (read this before quoting the corpus as evidence) ──────────────
//
// EMIT — the CHECKED-IN generated static parser (parse/gen_*_parser.av). Production.
// EXEC — the grammar tree INTERPRETER (features/grammar/executor.av). Still the
//        production parser for the TYPE family, so not a museum piece.
//
// There is NO hand row. It was retired in t-47hc.4.1 with the hand expression
// ladder itself (the decl hand leaf had already gone with `@hand(decl_hand)`), and
// the retirement was not optional: the "hand" env prefix differed from "emit" only
// in `AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_STATIC=0`, and once that variable stopped selecting anything,
// every `agree*` below would have compared the generated parser to ITSELF and passed
// by construction. That is the documented t-bw9s failure — a mode name outliving the
// implementation it named — and it is the exact shape this file exists to police.
//
// Historical note kept because the finding still holds: emit and hand AGREED across
// this whole corpus, including multi-error recovery, which is what made removing the
// interpreter re-parse fallback (and later the ladder) measurable rather than a leap.
// Comments below that read "emit-vs-hand" describe that era; the assertion they sit
// on now compares emit against exec.

extern fn avra_shell_exec(cmd: string) -> string
use @std.avrac.test_runner.{write_fixture}
use @std.avrac.parse.{parser_new}
use @std.avrac.core.{render_stmt_ids}

// The occurrence-ordered F-code signature of `bs2 check <src>` under `env`.
// Comparing the ORDERED list (duplicates preserved) captures both the error SET
// and the recovery ORDER + COUNT — a dropped or reordered error diverges.
fn errcodes(env: string, source: string) -> string {
    let path = "/tmp/avra_errdiff.av"
    write_fixture(path, source)
    avra_shell_exec("${env} ./build/bs2 check '${path}' 2>&1 | grep -oE 'F[0-9]{4}' | tr '\\n' ' '")
}

/// The EXPLICIT env prefix for a parser mode — every routing variable set, none inherited.
///
/// `parser_new` reads `decl_flip` and `decl_static` as `… != "0"` (ON by default) and
/// `no_static_fallback` as `== "1"` (OFF by default), so a prefix that sets only the ONE
/// variable distinguishing its mode is silently ambient-dependent: run the suite under
/// `AVRA_PARSER_DECL_FLIP=0` and the "emit" and production calls BOTH collapse to the
/// hand, and a three-path assertion passes having measured one implementation twice.
///
/// `rtree_at` already sets every field explicitly for exactly this reason, and says so —
/// "the vacuity class this file exists to police". This is its shell-invocation twin, and
/// the reasoning applies identically to the F-code oracles (`agree_all`) and the
/// message-level one (`msgs_agree_bad`); both route through here so the mapping lives in
/// ONE place. Under a run with none of the three set, every prefix below re-states the
/// existing default, so this is behaviour-preserving — it removes the ambient dependence,
/// not the meaning.
fn mode_env(mode: string) -> string {
    // EXPRESSION routing is pinned too, not just declaration routing: `expr_flip` is
    // read independently of the decl flags, so a prefix that sets only the decl
    // variable leaves the expression engine ambient.
    //
    // t-47hc.4.1 RETIRED the "hand" prefix. It used to read
    // `AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_STATIC=0 AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_FLIP=0`, which selected the hand
    // expression ladder — and that ladder is deleted, along with the variable that
    // chose it. Leaving the row in place would have made it byte-identical to "emit",
    // so every `agree*` in this file would compare the generated parser to ITSELF and
    // pass by construction: exactly the vacuity documented for `AVRA_PARSER_DECL_FLIP`
    // (t-bw9s), where a dead variable kept a mode name that no longer selected
    // anything. The surviving oracle pair is EMIT vs EXEC — two engines compiled from
    // one grammar, which is the pair that can actually drift.
    if mode == "exec" { return "AVRA_PARSER_DECL_STATIC=0 AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_FLIP=1" }
    if mode == "emit" { return "AVRA_PARSER_DECL_STATIC=1 AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_FLIP=0" }
    // production — the default routing, spelled out. Identical to "emit" today; kept
    // separate on purpose so a reintroduced fallback surfaces as a divergence between
    // the two rows rather than hiding behind a collapsed one.
    "AVRA_PARSER_DECL_STATIC=1 AVRA_PARSER_EXPR_FLIP=0"
}

/// The full diagnostic MESSAGES, not merely their F-codes (t-myz7, the `table` slice).
///
/// Everywhere else in this file the F-code signature discriminates, so `errcodes` is
/// enough. For the table literal it is NOT, and that is worth stating because the
/// difference is invisible until measured: `parse_table_literal`'s three semantic
/// aborts — duplicate column, row-too-short, row-too-long — ALL report under F0001 and
/// ALL cascade into the same F0013 `unexpected \`}\``. Their signatures are therefore
/// byte-identical (`F0001 F0013`), so an `agree_all` over codes stays green even if a
/// native rule fires the WRONG ONE of the three, or reports a generic alternation
/// failure carrying neither message. That is precisely the vacuity raised in review on
/// the F0040 case (#1157): an equal-and-nonzero signature is not evidence of the right
/// diagnostic.
///
/// ANSI is stripped before matching (`.\[…m`) because the renderer colourises every
/// diagnostic; the trailing `parse error at L:C:` summary is deliberately NOT used as
/// the source — the hand path prints it and the two native paths do not, which is a
/// difference in the final summary line, not in the diagnostics themselves.
fn errmsgs(env: string, source: string) -> string {
    let path = "/tmp/avra_errmsg.av"
    write_fixture(path, source)
    avra_shell_exec("${env} ./build/bs2 check '${path}' 2>&1 | sed 's/.\\[[0-9;]*m//g' | grep -oE 'error\\[F[0-9]+\\]: .*' | tr '\\n' '|'")
}

/// Do all THREE paths report the same diagnostic MESSAGES, and at least one of them?
/// The message-level sibling of `agree_all`. The nonempty check is the anti-vacuity
/// half — without it a fixture that stopped reporting entirely would "agree".
fn msgs_agree_bad(source: string) -> bool {
    let exec = errmsgs(mode_env("exec"), source)
    let emit = errmsgs(mode_env("emit"), source)
    let prod = errmsgs(mode_env("prod"), source)
    if exec == "" || emit != exec || prod != exec {
        // WHICH path diverged, and whether the capture came back EMPTY. `errmsgs` returns
        // filtered pipeline output and so cannot carry `bs2 check`'s exit status, which
        // leaves "the compiler died / the filter matched nothing" and "the messages really
        // differ" looking alike from the return value alone. They need different fixes, so
        // the printout separates them — the same idiom `tree_agrees_everywhere` and
        // `agree_tree` already use. No assertion can PASS on a lost status: an empty
        // capture fails the nonempty bar below, and `"".contains(…)` is false at the
        // three call sites that pin an exact message.
        println("three-path MESSAGE mismatch")
        println(source)
        println("exec=" + exec)
        println("emit=" + emit)
        println("prod=" + prod)
    }
    exec != "" && emit == exec && prod == exec
}

/// Does an F-code signature contain a PARSE-phase failure? Raised in review on the
/// `primary_base` corpus: `tree_agrees_everywhere` renders the tree AFTER
/// `parse_file_program()` has recovered, so all three paths can report and still agree,
/// and a fixture that never parsed would sail through. That is the same defect class as
/// the malformed anchor in `decl_flip_dispatch_test` (#1155).
///
/// The reviewer asked for ZERO diagnostics. Measured, that is not the right bar and it
/// would have failed on its own first run:
///
///   grammar { … } + full imports    F9005 F9001 F9001 F9001 F9005 F9005 F9003 …
///   grammar { … } w/o parse_grammar F3000   (the literal desugars to a call)
///   table { }                       (clean)
///
/// The warnings are incidental lint on the fixture, and F3000 is RESOLVE. Both run
/// after parsing and neither can say which parser answered — the same reasoning the
/// `decl_flip_dispatch_test` parse gate landed on. So the bar is: every path agrees,
/// AND the shared signature carries no parse-phase code (`F0001`–`F0999`, less the two
/// in that range that are not parse failures).
fn sig_has_parse_code(sig: string) -> bool {
    for c in sig.split(" ") {
        if c != "" && c.contains("F0") && c != "F0801" && c != "F0900" { return true }
    }
    false
}

fn static_raw(source: string) -> string { errcodes(mode_env("emit"), source) }
fn exec_oracle(source: string) -> string { errcodes(mode_env("exec"), source) }
// t-zrn3 — the THIRD path, and the one users actually run. `static_raw` and the TREE
// oracle below both disable the interpreter re-parse fallback, so between them they
// measure the EMIT-generated parser and nothing else. The DEFAULT path is static →
// had_error → re-parse with the INTERPRETER, so on every invalid input it is the
// EXECUTOR's recovery that ships — and an executor-only divergence was invisible to
// both oracles. It was real: exec_node FIRST-guarded a single-branch rule where emit
// runs it unconditionally, so every stray top-level token reported the alternation's
// generic F0001 instead of the hand parser's F0040, while both oracles stayed green.
fn default_path(source: string) -> string { errcodes(mode_env("prod"), source) }
fn agree(source: string) -> bool {
    let sr = static_raw(source)
    let ex = exec_oracle(source)
    if sr != ex {
        println("recovery diagnostic mismatch")
        println(source)
        println("emit=" + sr)
        println("exec=" + ex)
    }
    sr == ex
}
// ── PINNED expectations, for the inputs where the two engines legitimately differ ──
//
// The corpus is a differential, and a differential has one structural weakness: it
// says nothing about an input the two engines answer differently. Dropping such an
// input loses the coverage; asserting agreement on it is simply false. So those
// inputs are PINNED instead — the shipping parser's exact output, written out.
//
// A pinned expectation is strictly stronger than the differential where it applies
// (it cannot pass by both sides being wrong together, which is the vacuity `agree_bad`
// exists to catch), and strictly narrower (it says nothing about the other engine). It
// is the right tool only for a KNOWN divergence, and every use below names the reason.
//
// EVERY divergence pinned here is the same one: the grammar EXECUTOR is a weaker
// answerer for malformed DECLARATIONS than the emitted parser — CLAUDE.md records the
// executor no longer answering invalid declaration input at all, since the `@hand(decl_hand)`
// leaf that used to catch those went away. Where it still runs, it reports the enclosing
// alternation's failure IN ADDITION to the inner rule's, so its signature carries an
// extra F0001. That is a divergence in the engine that does NOT ship for declarations.
fn pin(source: string, expected: string) -> bool {
    let sr = static_raw(source)
    if sr != expected {
        println("PINNED recovery signature moved")
        println(source)
        println("expected=" + expected)
        println("actual  =" + sr)
    }
    sr == expected
}

// The TREE + diagnostic-COUNT twin of `pin`, for the same reason `agree_bad` is the
// tree twin of `agree`: an equal signature off a different tree is a real regression.
fn pin_tree(source: string, expected_tree: string, expected_diags: int) -> bool {
    let t = rtree(true, source)
    let d = rdiags(true, source)
    if t != expected_tree || d != expected_diags {
        println("PINNED recovery tree moved")
        println(source)
        println("expected=" + expected_tree + " diags=" + string(expected_diags))
        println("actual  =" + t + " diags=" + string(d))
    }
    t == expected_tree && d == expected_diags
}

// A malformed case that must ACTUALLY report an error: parser parity AND a
// nonempty F-code signature, so the assertion pins the `had_error` contract
// rather than passing vacuously when both parsers emit nothing.
fn agree_error(source: string) -> bool {
    let sr = static_raw(source)
    sr != "" && sr == exec_oracle(source)
}

// Agreement on ALL THREE parser paths at once: emit (fallback off), the DEFAULT
// production path (fallback on, so the executor answers), and hand. Used for the cases
// that characterise the executor's own recovery; `agree` stays emit-vs-hand so the
// existing corpus keeps measuring exactly what it always did.
fn agree_all(source: string) -> bool {
    let ex = exec_oracle(source)
    let sr = static_raw(source)
    let dflt = default_path(source)
    if sr != ex || dflt != ex {
        println("three-path recovery mismatch")
        println(source)
        println("emit=" + sr)
        println("default=" + dflt)
        println("exec=" + ex)
    }
    sr == ex && dflt == ex
}

spec "t-47hc.26: the native `if let` recovers exactly as the hand did" {
    // ROUTING GUARD. `if let` left `@hand(if_expr)` in t-47hc.26, and routing is
    // INVISIBLE ON VALID INPUT by construction: the native rule and the hand agree
    // there, so diff-test, the parity specs and the comptime smoke can all be green
    // while error recovery has silently regressed. These malformed forms are the only
    // witness, and each one corresponds to a `return .Err` in the hand's
    // parse_if_let_header — the three points the rule reproduces with `@require`.
    //
    // Written as `agree_bad` (F-code + diagnostic COUNT + tree) AND `agree_all` (all
    // three paths) per the routing discipline: the F-code oracles disable the fallback,
    // so on any invalid input the DEFAULT path is answered by the EXECUTOR, and an
    // executor-only divergence is invisible to them (t-zrn3).
    then "each malformed if-let reports, and recovers to the hand's tree" {
        // Binding absent (hand: consume_identifier fails), `=` absent (hand:
        // consume(Equal) fails), block absent (hand: the check(LeftBrace) guard), and
        // the subject missing entirely.
        agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let = x { 1 }\n r\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v x { 1 }\n r\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v = x 1\n r\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v =\n r\n}")
    }
    then "and all three parser paths agree on the recovered tree" {
        // The executor answers the default path on invalid input, so this is the
        // comparison the two F-code oracles structurally cannot make.
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let = x { 1 }\n r\n}") &&
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v x { 1 }\n r\n}") &&
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v = x 1\n r\n}") &&
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let v =\n r\n}")
    }
    then "a NESTED if-let is the case a build-minted temp would get wrong" {
        // Not a recovery case — an ORDERING case, and the reason `@gensym` is a
        // positional primary. The hand mints the OUTER temp first; a build-minted one
        // would number the inner binding first, because its sequence completes first.
        // Same tree from both parsers is the observable consequence.
        agree_all("fn f(a: int?, b: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let p = a { if let q = b { q } else { p } } else { 0 }\n r\n}")
    }
}

spec "ps3t.6.7 slice 5a: static parser error recovery == exec oracle (invalid corpus)" {
    // ps3t.6.7 slice 5b — the corpus GREW to a broad, category-diverse invalid set
    // (stray tokens, malformed decls of every keyword, control-flow, expressions,
    // multi-error, EOF-unclosed). All AGREE: static-raw error recovery is
    // byte-identical to hand across the board — the proof that gates slice 5c
    // (removing the interpreter re-parse fallback).
    then "stray top-level tokens: static-raw diagnostics match hand" {
        agree("fn a() -> int { 1 }\nthen\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n)\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n,\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("else\nfn b() -> int { 2 }")
    }
    // t-zrn3 — the same stray tokens as the block above, measured on ALL THREE paths.
    // The block above compares emit against hand and passed throughout the entire period
    // this was broken, because emit was never the one that was wrong. On the DEFAULT path
    // every one of these reported F0001 "unexpected token `X`" where the hand parser
    // reports F0040 "expected expression" — the executor's answer, shipped through the
    // re-parse fallback. `->` is included as the pure case: a token that starts nothing
    // at all, so it can only be answered by the tail.
    then "stray top-level tokens agree on the DEFAULT path too, not just emit-vs-hand" {
        agree_all("fn a() -> int { 1 }\nthen\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n)\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n,\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n->\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("else\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_all("->\nfn b() -> int { 2 }")
    }
    then "malformed function declarations" {
        agree("fn () -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a( -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a(x: ) -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }")
    }
    then "malformed type / enum / trait / impl declarations" {
        // PINNED, not agreed: the executor adds the enclosing alternation's own F0001
        // on top of the field rule's. See `pin`.
        pin("type T = { x: , y: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "F0001 F0013 ") &&
        agree("type T = { x: int\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E { A, , B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E A B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("trait T { fn m( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("impl for Foo { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // `shape` is the ONE decl keyword currently ROUTED onto the production path
    // (t-47hc slice 10.9, #1054), and until now the invalid corpus had no malformed
    // `shape` at all — so the recovery guard was blind to exactly the keyword whose
    // recovery had actually changed. Routing it looked clean partly because nothing
    // tested it broken. Mirrors the malformed-`type` cases above, since `shape` shares
    // `field` with it.
    then "malformed shape declarations (the routed keyword)" {
        // PINNED for the same reason as the `type` sibling — `shape` shares `field`.
        pin("shape S = { x: , y: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "F0001 F0013 ") &&
        agree("shape S = { x: int\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("shape = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("shape S = \nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("shape S { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // `use` and `mod` are the two decl keywords ROUTED onto the production path
    // (t-47hc slice 10.1) — and, exactly like `shape` above, they were routed with
    // ZERO malformed coverage here. Measuring them found EIGHT divergences, all of
    // them live on the production path (not just under the no-fallback oracle):
    // `use @.{x}` / `use @{x}` / `use @std.{}` / `use @std.{x,}` DROPPED hand's second
    // diagnostic; `use @std..{x}` / `use @std.{,x}` / `use @5.{x}` / `use @std.5.{x}`
    // ADDED a spurious one; `mod {` reported twice where hand reports once.
    //
    // The fix is `@try` on both branches: a routed rule that reports rolls back and
    // falls into `@hand(decl_hand)`, so the native path owns VALID input and hand still
    // owns recovery. These cases fail without it and pass with it.
    then "malformed use / mod declarations (the routed keywords)" {
        agree("use\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use foo\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use foo.{\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use .{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @.{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @std.{}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @std..{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @std.{x,}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @std.{,x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @5.{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("use @std.5.{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("mod\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("mod 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("mod {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The BINDING keywords join the routed set (t-47hc Phase 4: `let` / `mut` /
    // `const`, the highest-traffic residue of `@hand(decl_hand)` — every binding in
    // every body). Added BEFORE the routing per the discipline. Top-level and
    // in-body forms both, because the two dispatch through different loops; the
    // guard form's non-diverging else is the one coded diagnostic
    // (LetElseMustDiverge) the native builder must hand back rather than own.
    then "malformed bindings (the routed keywords), on all three paths" {
        agree_all("let\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let x =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let 5 = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let mut = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("mut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("mut y y = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("const\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The EMPTY TYPE ANNOTATION on all three binding keywords. `let x: = 1` was
        // here; `mut`/`const` were not, and both carried the same defect invisibly —
        // an un-`@require`d type position let the type view's generic F0040 "expected
        // expression" stand where the hand reports F0001 "expected type after `:`",
        // and the binding survived as `(… :<unknown> 1)` instead of aborting. Routing
        // `let` is what surfaced it; the siblings needed the same fix and now need the
        // same guard, or the next grammar edit can regress two of the three silently.
        agree_all("mut x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("const X: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("fn g() -> int {\n let a: = 1\n mut b: = 2\n const C: = 3\n return a\n}") &&
        agree_all("let (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("fn f() -> int {\n    let x = )\n    1\n}") &&
        agree_all("fn f(o: int?) -> int {\n    let v = o else { 1 }\n    v\n}") &&
        // The same keywords under the prefix wrappers, which this slice widens to
        // accept bindings (`exportable_inner` / `wrapped_inner`): a malformed
        // binding must fail the WHOLE wrapped branch back to the hand.
        agree_all("export let\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("export const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("/// d\nlet x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@a\nmut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The DESTRUCTURE form joins the routed set: every way its list, closer,
        // `=` or initializer can go missing, plus the `let mut (` spelling and
        // the export-wrapped variant.
        agree_all("let ( = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let (a, 5) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let (a, b = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let (a, b) t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let (a, b) =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("let mut (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("export let (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The prefix WRAPPERS join the routed set (t-47hc Phase 4: `///` doc, `//!` module
    // doc, `@annotation`; `export` went in #1098). Measured on ALL THREE paths, not
    // just emit-vs-hand: the wrappers are `@try`-routed, so on any malformed form the
    // branch rolls back and it is the EXECUTOR that answers the default path — the
    // divergence class the two-parser oracles are structurally blind to (t-zrn3).
    // The CONCURRENCY statements join the routed set (t-47hc Phase 4: `select` /
    // `parallel`). Added BEFORE the routing per the discipline. The select arm
    // list's hand recovery is distinctive — a malformed arm returns SILENTLY and
    // the caller's `}` consume does the reporting — so every partial-arm form is
    // pinned here: the native rule must hand all of them back.
    then "malformed select / parallel (the routed keywords), on all three paths" {
        agree_all("parallel\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("parallel x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { x <- }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { x <- ch }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { x <- ch -> }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("select { x <- ch -> { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // The SPEC-TEST blocks join the routed set (t-47hc Phase 4: `spec`, with its
    // `given` / `then` inner keywords). Added BEFORE the routing per the
    // discipline. The missing-name forms pin the silent-drop fix in
    // spec_test/parser.av: given/then bailed with a bare Err that
    // parse_spec_body swallowed, so those inputs used to parse CLEAN.
    then "malformed spec / given / then (the routed keyword), on all three paths" {
        agree_all("spec\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\"\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" { given }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" { given \"g\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" { then }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" { then \"t\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("spec \"s\" { given \"g\" { then } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // The COMPONENT definition forms (t-47hc Phase 4 slice A — corpus FIRST,
    // ahead of any routing). Every way the header, implements clause, config /
    // children schemas, `body: TokenStream` marker, and field decls can go
    // wrong, including the two coded diagnostics (implements-then-instance,
    // body: non-TokenStream). The instantiation forms are slices B/C.
    then "malformed component definitions (pre-routing corpus), on all three paths" {
        agree_all("component\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C implements {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C implements A B { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { config }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { config { x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { config { x: } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { children { x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // A NON-IDENT entry takes a different path from the three above: `x` is an
        // IDENT so the `@peek(not_rbrace) @gate` admits it and `config_entry` fails
        // inside, where `5` is declined by the gate itself. The gated element ABORTS,
        // and that is the hand's behaviour here — `parse_component_decl` propagates,
        // leaving the two stray `}`s for the caller (F0001 then F0013 F0013). It is
        // deliberately NOT `select`'s `@onfail(close)`: the two hand parsers disagree,
        // which is the whole reason the policy is per-rule and stated rather than
        // inferred (see GItem.onfail). Pinned because the resemblance to `select_decl`
        // invites exactly that inference — it has been proposed on review twice.
        agree_all("component C { config { 5 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { children { 5 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { body: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C { let x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C inst\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C implements A inst { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // ROUTING GUARD for t-47hc.5.1 slice B — the KEYWORD-PREFIXED INSTANTIATION
    // (`component C inst { … }`, parse_component_decl's third form). Landed
    // BEFORE the routing, per the slice-10 discipline: routing is invisible on
    // valid input by construction, so the invalid corpus is the only witness
    // that recovery did not silently change. `shape` shipped routed with no
    // malformed coverage (#1054) and had to be un-routed (#1059) — a keyword
    // must not be routed on no evidence.
    //
    // These pass TODAY (both paths reach the hand) and must go RED if the rule
    // is ever routed BARE, because the hand ABORTS a malformed item and lets
    // the statement-list loop synchronize, while a bare native branch is
    // error-TOLERANT and builds partial nodes. `@try` is what keeps them green.
    //
    // Cases are one per error site down the instantiation path. The three forms
    // slice A's corpus already covers (`component C inst`, `component C
    // implements A inst { }`, `component C 5`) are deliberately NOT repeated.
    then "malformed component INSTANTIATIONS (pre-routing corpus), on all three paths" {
        // config-pair sites (parse_comp_config_pairs)
        agree_all("component C inst { key = }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C inst { key = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // the instance block itself never closes
        agree_all("component C inst {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // nested-block site: a nested name with no `{` (parse_comp_block_body)
        agree_all("component C inst { nested name }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // method-shorthand site: the method body never closes. The `{` must stay
        // UNCLOSED — `run() { }` closes it, and then the only thing left wrong is
        // the outer block, which reports F0040 exactly like the bare case above.
        // Unterminated, this reports F0001 `expected `}` after method body`, which
        // is the site this case exists to cover.
        agree_all("component C inst { run() {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // body statement site: a `let` the block body cannot accept
        agree_all("component C inst { let x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // ── the four DECLINED arms (slice B2b corpus, landed before the routing) ──
        // parse_comp_block_body has four arms the native rule currently declines,
        // each of which ABORTS where a plain `declaration` would be happy. These
        // pin their malformed shapes NOW, while the hand still owns them, so the
        // guard exists before B2b routes them and can fail if B2b gets one wrong.
        // arm 2 — `ident name {` nested block, malformed statement INSIDE the body.
        // The braces stay balanced on purpose: an unterminated form (either
        // `nested name { }` or `nested name {`) just runs to EOF and reports the
        // same F0040 as the bare `component C inst {` case above, testing nothing
        // new. Balanced, it reaches the nested body and reports F0001 `expected
        // `=` after variable name` — arm 2's own path.
        agree_all("component C inst { nested name { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // arm 3 — the brace form (`ident {`, instance name == component name),
        // same reasoning. arm 2's OTHER site, a missing `{` after the nested name,
        // is already covered above by `component C inst { nested name }`.
        agree_all("component C inst { nested { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // arm 4 — method shorthand whose BODY carries a malformed statement
        agree_all("component C inst { run() { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // arm 1 — nested `component` with no name, and with no body
        agree_all("component C inst { component }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("component C inst { component D }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    then "malformed decl wrappers (the routed prefixes), on all three paths" {
        // t-47hc.5: was `native_agrees_hand_differs`. The hand parser is DELETED, so
        // there is no longer a hand answer to differ from — the divergence this pinned
        // ended with the oracle, not with a fix.
        agree_all("@\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@foo(\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@foo(,)\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@foo)\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@foo") &&
        agree_all("/// doc\nfn ( ) -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("//! mdoc\n* 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("/// doc\n@foo(\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_all("@foo\nexport enum { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // `enum` joins the routed set (t-47hc slice 10.11). It is the first routed keyword
    // with real INTERNAL structure — a type-param list, a variant list, and payload
    // fields — so the forms that can go wrong are not just "the name is missing": a
    // half-closed generic list, a variant with an empty payload field, a non-IDENT where
    // a variant belongs. Two malformed enums were already in the blocks above; these
    // widen the coverage to the shapes routing actually reaches, BEFORE trusting it.
    then "malformed enum declarations (the routed keyword)" {
        agree("enum { A, B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E { A,\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E { A(x: ) }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E { A( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E< { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E<T { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("enum E { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // `type` joins the routed set (t-47hc slice 10.12) — the last and largest of the
    // decl keywords, and the one that needed two fixes before it could go: the RHS forms
    // made exclusive (t-myz7.1) and the executor's missing unguarded tail (t-zrn3). The
    // second one is why `type T =` followed by a `fn` declaration is here: `tok_starts_type`
    // includes `fn`, so the alias branch is entered on the following declaration's lead
    // token and the remainder becomes a stray-token item — the exact shape that exposed
    // the executor gap. It is measured on all three paths by the block above.
    then "malformed type declarations (the routed keyword)" {
        // The bare-RHS EDGE, on all three paths. `type T =` with nothing after it is
        // ACCEPTED — by the HAND parser too, which is what makes it parity rather than a
        // hole; diagnostic_equivalence_test pins the node both build. What varies is the
        // FOLLOWER: `let`/EOF leave it alone, `mod`/`use` are consumed as the alias and
        // fail in the resolver, and `}` / `)` fail in the parser. Each of those is the
        // hand's own behaviour, reproduced. (Raised in review on #1068 as a possible
        // hole — measured instead of argued, and kept as coverage.)
        agree_all("type T =") &&
        agree_all("type T =\nlet q = 1\n") &&
        agree_all("type T =\nmod foo\n") &&
        agree_all("type T =\n}\n") &&
        agree_all("type T =\n)\n") &&
        agree("type T =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type T< = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type T<A = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type T { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type T = int without\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("type T = int only { \nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The LAST four decl keywords (t-47hc slice 10.13): `fn`, `extern`, `trait`, `impl`.
    // Some of these forms were already in the blocks above as generic malformed-decl
    // cases; they are repeated here deliberately, grouped by the keyword that now owns
    // them, because that is the granularity a future un-routing would need. The added
    // ones probe what routing actually reaches: a truncated generic list, an empty
    // parameter, a bodyless `extern`, a member list that never closes.
    then "malformed fn / extern / trait / impl declarations (the routed keywords)" {
        agree("fn () -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a( -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a(x: ) -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a() -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("fn a<T() -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("extern fn () -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("extern fn a(\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("extern a() -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree("trait T { fn m( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("trait { fn m() }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("trait T {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("trait T { type }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("impl for Foo { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("impl Foo {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree("impl Foo { fn m( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    then "a malformed TYPE-PARAMETER list on each routed keyword agrees" {
        // The corpus had malformed BODIES for every routed keyword and malformed
        // type-parameter lists for none of `trait` / `shape` / `type` / `impl` — so the
        // one construct every one of them shares was measured on exactly one of them
        // (`fn a<T(`, above). Sweeping it across the family is what found `trait T<`,
        // which reported TWO diagnostics natively (`expected \`IDENT\`` then
        // `expected \`>\``) where the hand reports one, because the native rule parses
        // the list the hand blindly skips.
        //
        // `agree_all`, not `agree`: the executor answers the default path on invalid
        // input, and the emit-vs-hand comparison is blind to it (t-zrn3).
        agree_error("trait T< { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("trait T< { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("trait T<X { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("trait T<X { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("enum E< { A }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("enum E< { A }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("enum E<,T> { A }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("enum E<,T> { A }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("shape S< = { x: int }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("shape S< = { x: int }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("type T< = { x: int }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("type T< = { x: int }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("impl F< { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("impl F< { }\nfn zz() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    then "malformed bindings and control-flow statements" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let x = \n return 1\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let = 5\n return 1\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n const C\n return 1\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n if { return 1 }\n return 2\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n while return 2\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n for x { return 3 }\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n match { 1 -> 2 }\n}")
    }
    then "malformed expressions: dangling operators and unclosed delimiters" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let x = 1 +\n return x\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let y = (1 + 2\n return y\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let w = foo(1,\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.\n return 0\n}") &&
        // t-47hc.4.3 — `agree_all`, not `agree`: this input rides the ONE
        // `@gate @onfail(bail)` shape in the grammar (`return_stmt`), and the two engines
        // did NOT agree on it. Emit's `gated_first_check` stands down for `bail` (the
        // callee owns the message) while the executor's `gated_first_ok` stood down only
        // for `skip`/`close`, so the pre-check fired there and answered F0001 where the
        // hand and emit both answer F0040 — same wording, different CODE. `agree` compares
        // emit against the hand and so was blind to it by construction, which is the
        // t-zrn3 blind spot exactly. Measured with a control: revert the executor's
        // stand-down and this line goes red.
        agree_all("fn f() -> int {\n return * 3\n}")
    }
    then "the trailing dot agrees on ALL THREE paths, executor included" {
        // The `.`-led `@fallback` that replaced `@hand(postfix_suffix)` (t-47hc.33.2)
        // owns this diagnostic natively now, and the two engines reach it differently:
        // emit REPORTS then still builds `mk_field_access` from the cursor token, while
        // the executor's `@require` aborts to `error_node_silent()`. The corpus pinned
        // this input emit-vs-hand (`agree` above) and tree-vs-hand (`agree_bad` below),
        // but never against the EXECUTOR — and the default production path is answered by
        // the executor on invalid input, so an executor-only divergence here was
        // structurally invisible (the t-zrn3 blind spot). `agree_all` is the third
        // comparison; without it, "all paths agree on the trailing dot" was an
        // assumption, not a measurement.
        // `agree_error` FIRST on each input, then `agree_all`: on its own `agree_all`
        // only checks that the three signatures MATCH, so it passes vacuously if every
        // path silently accepts a malformed input — three empty strings agree. Pairing
        // it with the non-empty check is this file's own rule (the `agree_bad`
        // docstring), and the reason is the `shape` regression: a guard that cannot
        // fail is worth nothing.
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.\n return 0\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.\n return 0\n}") &&
            agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.b.\n return 0\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.b.\n return 0\n}")
        // `a?.` is DELIBERATELY absent: it genuinely diverges (native F0001 vs hand
        // F0040) and the divergence PREDATES the `.` fallback — the pre-#1078 compiler
        // reports F0001 too. Both `?.` branches are `@peek`-gated on a valid FIELDNAME
        // (`qdot_method_call` / `qdot_plain_field`), so a trailing `?.` satisfies
        // neither and no branch owns the diagnostic; the `.` family got a `@fallback`
        // in t-47hc.33.2 and the `?.` family never did. Filed as its own gap rather
        // than asserted here, so this spec does not fail for something it is not
        // about. Adding `agree_all("… a?. …")` is the failing-first witness when
        // someone fixes it.
    }
    then "a GATED member/field list agrees on ALL THREE paths, executor included" {
        // The gated call's FIRST re-check (t-myz7). `@gate` REPLACES a group's FIRST with
        // the preceding `@peek` cond — that is what lets the loop enter where the
        // element's own FIRST would refuse (`}` / EOF), so the hand's "report about the
        // ELEMENT" recovery is reproducible. The price is that the callee's standing
        // promise, "my caller proved my FIRST", no longer holds, so the gated call has to
        // re-check it itself. emit has done that since the gate landed
        // (`gated_first_check`); the INTERPRETER never did and simply fell into the
        // sub-rule.
        //
        // `trait T {` therefore reached the EXPRESSION tail on the default path and said
        // F0040 `expected expression` where the hand names the missing member, and
        // `shape S = { x: int` answered with three diagnostics to the hand's one — the
        // loop entered `field` standing on `fn`, `field` reported a missing name then a
        // missing `:`, and `shape_decl` then reported a missing `}`.
        //
        // Every input here ALREADY sits in the `agree` corpus above and passed there
        // throughout, because `agree` compares emit against hand and emit was never the
        // one that was wrong. The default production path answers invalid input with the
        // EXECUTOR, so only `agree_all` can see this — the t-zrn3 blind spot, in the
        // keyword family that has the most gates. `agree_error` first on each, per this
        // file's rule that a signature check must also prove the signature is non-empty.
        agree_error("trait T {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("trait T {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("shape S = { x: int\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("shape S = { x: int\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("enum E {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("enum E {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("enum E { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("enum E { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    then "an aborted declaration does not trail a generic CapVal-coercion diagnostic" {
        // The boundary now carries EVERY diagnostic the DSL run recorded, not just
        // `diags[0]` (t-myz7 — a committed native rule reports more than once, and the
        // one-diagnostic seam made the hand's multi-error recovery unreachable no matter
        // what the grammar said). The four `capval_to_*` coercions are what that
        // exposed: they run on the run's RESULT, so their generic "expected a
        // statement" / "expected a field" was appended AFTER every real diagnostic and
        // the seam had been dropping it silently.
        //
        // A coercion failure is a real finding on a CLEAN parse (a grammar or builder
        // bug) and pure noise on a FAILED one — the rule aborted, so of course it handed
        // back `Nil`. Without that distinction every one of these gained a spurious
        // trailing F0001 apiece the moment the boundary widened. `agree_all`, because
        // the coercion sits at the boundary itself and so fires on all three native
        // paths at once.
        agree_error("use\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("use\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("mod\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("mod\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("const\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("const\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("export const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("export const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_error("shape = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("shape = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    then "multi-error recovery: static-raw finds the SAME ordered error set as hand" {
        // Each program has 3-6 parse errors; recovery must resynchronise and find
        // every one, in the same order, under both parsers.
        agree("fn a() -> int { 1 + }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }\nthen\nfn c() -> int { * 3 }") &&
        // PINNED: contains the `{ x: , … }` field abort, so it carries the executor's
        // extra alternation F0001 exactly as the single-error case above does.
        pin("type T = { x: , y: }\nfn f(a: , b: int) -> int { return a }\nfn g() -> int { let z = ; return z }",
            "F0001 F0013 F0001 F0013 F0001 F0041 ") &&
        agree("fn h() -> int {\n  if { return 1 }\n  while return 2\n  for x { return 3 }\n}")
    }
    then "unclosed constructs at EOF" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n return 1\n") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let x = (1 + 2 * 3\n") &&
        agree("type T = {\n x: int,\n")
    }
    then "a valid program produces no diagnostics under either mode (sanity)" {
        static_raw("fn ok() -> int { 1 + 2 }") == "" && exec_oracle("fn ok() -> int { 1 + 2 }") == ""
    }
}

// ── t-47hc.3 acceptance item 3 — the RECOVERY-TREE oracle ─────────────────────
//
// The F-code oracle above is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT to retire the
// interpreter re-parse fallback (the "5c lesson", commit e1a55fef): two parsers
// can emit the SAME ordered F-codes while recovering DIFFERENT trees. This oracle
// closes that blind spot — it compares the rendered whole-program recovery TREE
// (the `.Ok`/`.Err` distinction surfaces as top-level rows; the recovered
// Stmt/Expr placeholder shape rides in the same string) of the STATIC generated
// parser (interpreter fallback OFF) against the hand oracle. In-process via the
// parser's mode fields — no `bs2` invocation. `render_stmt_ids` is TOTAL and
// renders the recovery placeholders distinctly (`Stmt.Error`/`Expr.Error` →
// `(error)`, a failed expr-stmt → `(expr (error))`), so one string equality
// captures both the outcome AND the shape.

/// The whole-program tree under ONE parser ENGINE — the core `rtree` and
/// `tree_agrees_everywhere` both read, so the engine selection lives in one place.
/// `mode`: "emit" (the CHECKED-IN generated parser) | "exec" (the grammar EXECUTOR).
/// Those are the two surviving implementations; production is "emit", so a comparison
/// that omits "exec" cannot see an executor-only divergence — and the executor is still
/// the production parser for the TYPE family, so it is not a museum piece.
fn rtree_at(mode: string, source: string) -> string {
    mut p = parser_new(source)
    // EVERY routing field is set explicitly, for BOTH modes. `parser_new` reads
    // `decl_static` from the environment (`… != "0"`, so ON by default) and `expr_flip`
    // independently of it, which means a helper that sets only the ONE field
    // distinguishing its mode is silently ambient-dependent — and the two modes can
    // collapse onto one engine while `tree_agrees_everywhere` passes comparing it
    // against itself. That is the vacuity class this file exists to police: asserting
    // two implementations agree while running one is worse than not asserting it.
    let f = mode_flags(mode)
    p.decl_static = f.decl_static
    p.expr_flip = f.expr_flip
    let r = p.parse_file_program()
    if r.ids == null { return "<null>" }
    render_stmt_ids(r.store, r.ids!)
}

/// The IN-PROCESS twin of `mode_env` — EVERY routing field for the named mode, so a
/// helper cannot inherit one from `parser_new`'s defaults. Returned as VALUES rather
/// than applied here because a plain fn's `p` is immutable; the decision is what needs
/// centralizing, and each caller assigns onto its own `mut p`.
///
/// The expression field is pinned as well as the declaration one, and that is not
/// optional: `expr_flip` is read independently of the decl flags, so a helper that sets
/// only `decl_static` leaves the expression engine ambient and both "sides" can end up
/// on the same one.
///
///   emit — the CHECKED-IN generated static parser (decl + expr)
///   exec — the Grammar-tree interpreter (decl + expr)
///
/// There is no "hand" row: t-47hc.4.1 deleted the hand expression ladder, and the decl
/// hand leaf went with `@hand(decl_hand)` before it. A mode name that selects neither is
/// worse than no mode at all (t-bw9s), so it is gone rather than aliased onto "emit".
type RouteFlags = { decl_static: bool, expr_flip: bool }

fn mode_flags(mode: string) -> RouteFlags {
    if mode == "emit" {
        return RouteFlags { decl_static: true, expr_flip: false }
    }
    RouteFlags { decl_static: false, expr_flip: true }
}


fn rtree(static_raw: bool, source: string) -> string {
    // static-raw: the EMIT-generated parser. Otherwise the interpreter engine — the
    // second implementation of the same grammar, and the only remaining oracle.
    rtree_at(if static_raw { "emit" } else { "exec" }, source)
}
fn agree_tree(source: string) -> bool {
    let sr = rtree(true, source)
    let ex = rtree(false, source)
    if sr != ex {
        println("recovery tree mismatch")
        println(source)
        println("emit=" + sr)
        println("exec=" + ex)
    }
    sr == ex
}

// ── t-47hc.4.3 — the ONE input where the two engines deliberately DIVERGE ─────
//
// Every other oracle in this file asserts emit == exec. This one asserts the
// opposite, for a single input, and it is recorded here rather than dropped from the
// corpus because a case removed is a case nobody re-examines.
//
// The input is a BARE `@`. The hand's `parse_annotations` takes `current_lexeme` as the
// annotation NAME without ever requiring an identifier, so `@` followed by `fn` builds an
// annotation *named* `fn`, then reads `f() -> int { 1 }` as a call expression and reports
// a stray `}`. Its tree is `(@annotated @fn  (expr (call f ()))) (error)`: a fabricated
// annotation that has swallowed a valid function declaration whole.
//
// Reproducing that is NOT a matter of loosening the name slot. The fabricated name is
// only the first half; the second is the declaration being re-read as an expression
// statement, and matching it would mean changing how EVERY annotated declaration parses
// in order to preserve one broken recovery. Measured, not assumed — see the ticket.
//
// So the native engines report the bad `@` once and let the function parse:
// `(error) (fn f () -> int (expr 1))`. This asserts what must actually hold — the native
// paths agree with EACH OTHER, the input still reports, and the hand still differs. That
// last clause is what stops this being a guard that cannot fail: fix the hand and this
// goes RED, which is the prompt to fold the case back into `agree_all`/`agree_bad`.
// ── t-47hc.5 → t-47hc.4.1: THE VACUITY IS OVER. What this block used to say ──
//
// It read "THIS CORPUS IS CURRENTLY VACUOUS", on the grounds that `parse_declaration_hand`
// was deleted, so `exec_oracle()` and `static_raw()` ran the SAME engine and every
// `agree` / `agree_all` / `agree_bad` / `agree_tree` assertion below passed by
// construction — "all ~525 of them". That was TRUE of the routing it described: both
// legs were `AVRA_PARSER_DECL_STATIC=1` with the expression flags deciding nothing.
//
// It is NOT true now, and the retraction is kept rather than the text replaced, because
// the reason it stopped being true is the useful part. t-47hc.4.1 repointed the two legs
// at EMIT and EXEC — the generated static parser and the tree interpreter, two engines
// compiled from one grammar (see the header). They are not the same engine, and ten
// inputs proved it on the first run: nine where the executor answers a malformed
// declaration with an extra alternation diagnostic (now PINNED, see `pin`), and one
// genuine bug — the executor had no `cell_mode` guard on its binary ladder, so a table
// cell swallowed the next row's leading `-`.
//
// So the assertions below are LIVE. The spec that follows used to pin the vacuity; it now
// pins the three inputs that motivated it, as ordinary agreement checks.
spec "t-47hc.4.1: emit and exec agree on the inputs that split the hand from the native paths" {
    then "a bare `@`, a malformed binding, and a malformed wrapper agree across engines" {
        // Three inputs the hand and the native paths demonstrably DISAGREED on while the
        // hand existed: a bare `@` (the hand invented an annotation named `fn`), a
        // malformed binding, and a malformed wrapper. With the hand gone they are
        // ordinary two-engine agreement checks — and they are not vacuous, because emit
        // and exec DO diverge elsewhere in this file (that is what `pin` exists for).
        exec_oracle("@\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") == static_raw("@\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            exec_oracle("let x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") == static_raw("let x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            exec_oracle("@a\nmut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") == static_raw("@a\nmut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
}

// t-47hc.4.1 — `native_agrees_hand_differs` and `native_tree_agrees_hand_differs` are
// DELETED here. Both were "the native paths agree with each other AND the hand differs"
// helpers, for cases the hand answered differently on purpose. Neither had a caller left,
// and both had rotted into checks that could not fail: the F-code one compared
// `static_raw` against `default_path`, which `mode_env` maps to the same string, and the
// tree one re-read `rtree_at("exec", …)` for its "does the third path still differ?"
// clause after already establishing equality with that value. Raised in review on PR
// #1220. A guard that cannot fail is worth less than no guard, because it reads as
// coverage — so they go rather than get renamed. Their ROLE survives as `pin` /
// `pin_tree` / `pin_pos` above, which assert the shipping parser's exact output for a
// known divergence instead of asserting that a divergence exists.

// ── t-6u9b — the ANTI-VACUITY half: diagnostic COUNT ──────────────────────────
//
// Every oracle above compares the two parsers to EACH OTHER. That is exactly the
// right shape for catching DRIFT, and exactly the wrong shape for catching a bug
// they SHARE: when both paths silently drop the same token, their trees agree,
// their F-codes agree (both empty), and the spec passes — vacuously — while the
// compiler accepts text it discarded.
//
// This is not hypothetical. `let x = .(1)` sat in the corpus below reporting ZERO
// diagnostics on BOTH paths: `parse_primary` consumed the `.` for the contextual
// enum-variant shorthand, found no identifier, and FELL THROUGH with the token
// already eaten — so `(1)` parsed as a plain group and the `.` vanished. `agree_tree`
// was green on it the whole time. Only counting diagnostics on known-invalid input
// finds that class, which is why `agree_bad` — not `agree_tree` — is the default
// assertion for a malformed source now.
//
// (`agree_error` above pins the same non-vacuity via `bs2 check`'s F-code string.
// This is its in-process twin: no fixture write, no subprocess, so it costs nothing
// to apply to the WHOLE corpus rather than a hand-picked handful.)

/// Diagnostic count for `source` under one parser mode — same mode selection as `rtree`.
// ── the SrcPos side-table oracle (t-47hc.5.1) ────────────────────────────────
//
// THE GATE NOTHING ELSE PROVIDES. Every other oracle in this file is blind to
// per-statement POSITIONS: `errcodes` compares F-codes, `rtree` compares tree
// SHAPE, `rdiags` compares a COUNT — and diff-test compares IR, which SrcPos
// does not reach because it only affects diagnostics. So a native rule that
// dropped or misplaced `record_pos` would leave the entire gate set green.
//
// That is not hypothetical: the hand's `push_body_elem` (component_decl/parser.av)
// stamps every component-body row via `store.stmts.record_pos`, and GAP 1 of the
// zero-@hand plan is precisely that grammar actions do not do this for free. Any
// slice that makes a statement-containing form native has to spend
// `mk_stamp_stmt_at` correctly, and until now nothing could tell whether it had.
//
// Dumps `line:col` for every STAMPED statement row, in id order. Both parsers
// build the same tree (agree_tree pins that separately), so id order is
// comparable.
//
// Only stamped rows (`line > 0`) are compared, and that filter is load-bearing
// rather than cosmetic. A `@try` branch that rolls back leaves its allocated
// rows behind as ORPHANS — unreferenced by the tree, never stamped. The hand
// never speculates, so it never has any, and a raw all-rows dump reports the
// difference as a position divergence when nothing about positions differs.
// Measured on `component C inst { let x }`:
//     hand   = 0:0 1:1 2:17
//     static = 0:0 0:0 1:1 2:17    <-- one orphan from the rolled-back @try
// The trees and the IR are identical there (an unreferenced row codegens
// nothing); only the arena differs. `record_pos` itself ignores `line <= 0`, so
// an unstamped row carries no position to compare in the first place.
//
// A genuinely DROPPED position still fails: the row vanishes from the stamped
// sequence, so the two lists diverge in length and content.
fn poslist(static_raw: bool, source: string) -> string {
    mut p = parser_new(source)
    let f = mode_flags(if static_raw { "emit" } else { "exec" })
    p.decl_static = f.decl_static
    p.expr_flip = f.expr_flip
    let r = p.parse_file_program()
    if r.ids == null { return "<null>" }
    mut out = ""
    mut i = 0
    while i < r.store.stmts.count() {
        let sp = r.store.stmts.pos_of(StmtId(i))
        if sp.line > 0 { out = out + string(sp.line) + ":" + string(sp.col) + " " }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

fn rdiags(static_raw: bool, source: string) -> int {
    mut p = parser_new(source)
    let f = mode_flags(if static_raw { "emit" } else { "exec" })
    p.decl_static = f.decl_static
    p.expr_flip = f.expr_flip
    let r = p.parse_file_program()
    r.bag.diagnostics.length
}

/// The assertion for an INVALID source: both parsers recover to the same tree AND
/// both actually REPORT, the same number of times. Use this rather than `agree_tree`
/// for anything malformed — `agree_tree` alone cannot tell "reported and recovered"
/// from "silently dropped".
fn agree_bad(source: string) -> bool {
    if !agree_tree(source) { return false }
    let sr = rdiags(true, source)
    let ex = rdiags(false, source)
    if sr == 0 || ex == 0 {
        println("SILENT DROP — invalid source parsed with no diagnostic")
        println(source)
        println("emit=${string(sr)} exec=${string(ex)}")
        return false
    }
    if sr != ex {
        println("diagnostic COUNT divergence emit=${string(sr)} exec=${string(ex)}")
        println(source)
        return false
    }
    true
}

spec "t-47hc.3: recovery-TREE shape == exec oracle (invalid corpus)" {
    // The same category-diverse invalid corpus as the F-code oracle, now asserting
    // the RECOVERED TREES match — a strictly stronger check. Every case here agrees
    // at the tree level (the single known divergence is characterised separately
    // below), so this is the green gate that grows as leaf forms migrate.
    then "stray top-level tokens recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int { 1 }\nthen\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n)\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n,\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        // `* 5` (a binary-operator-led expr-stmt) was the one 5c divergence — the
        // static parse_expr_stmt now yields Stmt.Error on a failed expression, so
        // it recovers to the same bare `(error)` as hand (folded in from the
        // former characterisation spec once the emit-error fix landed).
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("else\nfn b() -> int { 2 }")
    }
    then "malformed declarations recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn () -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a( -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a(x: ) -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a() -> { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        // PINNED (see `pin`): the executor answers this field abort with an extra
        // alternation diagnostic, so the COUNT half of `agree_bad` cannot hold.
        pin_tree("type T = { x: , y: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "(error)\n(fn f () -> int (expr 1))", 2) &&
        agree_bad("type T = { x: int\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E { A, , B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E A B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("trait T { fn m( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("impl for Foo { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The TREE half of the routed-keyword coverage. The F-code oracle above catches a
    // dropped or added diagnostic; this catches the shape of what was BUILT — a routed
    // rule that reports and then builds a partial node (a nameless `use`, a segment-less
    // `mod`) has the same F-codes as hand's `(error)` and a different tree. Both halves
    // are needed, which is the whole lesson of the `shape` regression.
    then "malformed use / mod recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("use\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use foo\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use foo.{\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use @.{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use @std.{}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use @std..{x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("use @std.{,x}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("mod\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("mod 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("mod {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // t-47hc PHASE 4 PREREQUISITE — the DECL WRAPPERS, added BEFORE any of them is
    // routed.
    //
    // All nine decl keywords are native; what keeps `@hand(decl_hand)` alive is the
    // prefix-wrapper family (`///` doc, `//!` module doc, `@annotation`, `export`) plus
    // the IDENT-led `Comp inst { … }`. Those wrappers are RECURSIVE — each parses its
    // prefix and calls `parse_declaration` again — so routing them moves every
    // declaration in the file onto the native path, the same blast radius the
    // body-bearing keywords had in 10.13.
    //
    // NOT ONE OF THESE FORMS WAS IN THE CORPUS. That is the exact condition the routing
    // discipline names as fatal: routing is invisible on VALID input by construction
    // (the native rule and the hand agree there — that is what parity work buys), so a
    // construct absent from the invalid corpus gets routed on no evidence at all.
    // `shape` shipped that way in #1054 and had to be un-routed in #1059; `use`/`mod`
    // shipped that way and diverged on 8 of 18 malformed forms.
    //
    // These pass TODAY on the hand path. Their job is to fail loudly the moment a
    // wrapper is routed and its recovery does not match — which is the only witness
    // that exists for that class of regression.
    // t-47hc Phase 4, routing `let` / `mut` / `const`: the tree half of the binding
    // corpus above. A routed rule that reports and then builds a partial binding (a
    // nameless `let`, an initializer-less `mut`) has the same F-codes as hand's
    // `(error)` and a different tree — the exact `shape`/`use`/`mod` lesson.
    then "malformed bindings recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("let\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let x =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let 5 = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let mut = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("mut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("mut y y = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("const\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The empty-type-annotation trio (see the F-code spec above): the TREE is the
        // half that catches "reported the right message and kept the binding anyway".
        agree_bad("mut x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("const X: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn g() -> int {\n let a: = 1\n mut b: = 2\n const C: = 3\n return a\n}") &&
        agree_bad("let (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n    let x = )\n    1\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f(o: int?) -> int {\n    let v = o else { 1 }\n    v\n}") &&
        agree_bad("export let\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("export const C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("/// d\nlet x: = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("@a\nmut\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let ( = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let (a, 5) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let (a, b = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let (a, b) t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let (a, b) =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("let mut (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("export let (a b) = t\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The tree half of the select / parallel corpus above.
    // The tree half of the select / parallel corpus above. `select { x }` is the
    // case that caught a real hand bug pre-routing: the arm loop bailed on a
    // missing `<-` with the binding already consumed and NO report, so the input
    // parsed clean with the arm silently dropped (fixed in select_stmt/parser.av
    // alongside this corpus; agree_bad's silent-drop guard is what saw it).
    then "malformed select / parallel recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("parallel\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("parallel x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select x\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { x <- }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { x <- ch }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { x <- ch -> }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("select { x <- ch -> { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // The tree half of the spec / given / then corpus above.
    then "malformed spec / given / then recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("spec\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\"\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" { given }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" { given \"g\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" { then }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" { then \"t\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("spec \"s\" { given \"g\" { then } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // The tree half of the component-definition corpus above.
    then "malformed component definitions recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("component\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C implements {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C implements A B { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { config }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { config { x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { config { x: } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { children { x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The TREE half of the non-IDENT entry (see the F-code spec above) — the half
        // that catches "reported the right sequence and recovered somewhere else".
        agree_bad("component C { config { 5 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { children { 5 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { body: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C { let x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C implements A inst { }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // The TREE half of the instantiation corpus above. This is the half that
    // actually caught the enum_decl routing regression (t-myz7.2): the F-code
    // signatures can agree while the RECOVERED TREE diverges, because the hand
    // aborts to Stmt.Error and synchronizes where a tolerant native branch
    // builds a partial node. agree_bad also refuses a SILENT DROP — invalid
    // source that parses with no diagnostic on either path.
    // THE HIGHEST-VALUE ASSERTIONS IN THE SLICE. These four are VALID input —
    // they parse clean, so they cannot live in `agree_bad`, which requires a
    // diagnostic. They are the exact cases a TOKEN-LEVEL rewrite of the config
    // -pair peeks would get wrong, and the reason those scans are shared cores
    // (chars.av) rather than re-derived: the native rule and the hand must
    // reach the same verdict on each, and the tree is the proof.
    //
    //   -5          the number test reads the first BYTE, so this is NOT a
    //               config value — it falls through and `key -5` becomes a
    //               binary expression statement in the block body
    //   /* c */     the scans skip whitespace ONLY, never comments, so the
    //               string never registers as a value
    //   r"s"        a raw string leads with `r`, not `"`
    //   trueish     the keyword test is a PREFIX match, so this DOES read as
    //               a config pair whose value is the identifier `trueish`
    then "the four config-pair divergences parse identically to the hand" {
        agree_tree("component C inst { key -5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { key /* c */ \"v\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { key r\"s\" }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { key trueish }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    // These four were ONCE declined by the native rule and handed back to the hand.
    // They are ROUTED now — `comp_block_item` carries all four arms (raw-named,
    // raw-bare, nested-named, nested-bare, method shorthand) with `declaration` as the
    // honest unguarded tail. Measured: the four forms compile with BOTH production
    // leaves refused (`AVRA_REFUSE_HAND_LEAF='*'`), 0 refusals.
    //
    // The assertions stay exactly as they are. They were written as a
    // declined-vs-hand parity check and they now serve as the routed-vs-hand parity
    // check — the same claim either way, and the one that would go red if a future
    // change to those arms drifted from `parse_comp_block_body`.
    then "PROBE: a native instantiation BODY survives (statements not dropped)" {
        agree_tree("component C inst { key \"v\"\n  let z = 1\n  foo()\n}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    then "the declined body forms still match the hand exactly" {
        agree_tree("component C inst { nested name { } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { nested { } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { run() { } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("component C inst { component D e { } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    then "malformed component INSTANTIATIONS recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("component C inst { key = }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { key = 1\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { nested name }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { run() {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { let x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The TREE half of the four declined arms — the half that actually caught
        // the enum_decl routing regression (t-myz7.2): F-codes can agree while the
        // recovered tree diverges, because the hand aborts to Stmt.Error and
        // synchronizes where a tolerant native branch builds a partial node.
        agree_bad("component C inst { nested name { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { nested { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { run() { let x } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { component }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("component C inst { component D }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }

    then "malformed decl WRAPPERS recover to the same tree" {
        // Every form here was checked to actually REPORT on both paths before being
        // added. An earlier draft of this block also listed `export\nfn f()`,
        // `export let x = 1` and `/// orphan doc\nfn f()` as malformed — they are
        // VALID (the wrapper attaches to the declaration on the next line), so
        // `agree_bad`, which requires both parsers to report, failed on them. The
        // parsers were right and the corpus entry was wrong; those moved to the
        // valid-input tree pin below.
        agree_bad("export export fn g() -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // A bare `@`, an unterminated arg list, an empty arg, a stray close paren.
        // t-47hc.5: was `native_tree_agrees_hand_differs` — see the note in the
        // three-path spec above.
        agree_bad("@\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("@foo(\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("@foo(,)\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("@foo)\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // An annotation with NOTHING following it — the wrapper's recursive call into
        // `parse_declaration` hits EOF, which is the edge a routed rule is most likely
        // to get wrong (it must abort, not build a wrapper around nothing).
        agree_bad("@foo") &&
        // A malformed INNER declaration under a wrapper: the diagnostic must come from
        // the inner decl, not be swallowed by the prefix that wraps it.
        agree_bad("export enum { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // ...and under a STACK of wrappers, since they nest by recursion.
        agree_bad("@foo\nexport enum { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // t-47hc Phase 4, routing `///` / `//!` / `@annotation`: the doc-led halves of
        // the same classes — a malformed inner under a doc line, a module doc followed
        // by a stray token (the wrapper must not swallow it), and a malformed
        // annotation under a doc line (the stacked-wrapper rollback path).
        agree_bad("/// doc\nfn ( ) -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("//! mdoc\n* 5\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("/// doc\n@foo(\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The VALID half of the wrapper family. `agree_bad` cannot cover these — nothing
    // reports — but routing must preserve their SHAPE, and a wrapper that silently
    // stopped attaching to the following declaration would be a wrong-tree bug with no
    // diagnostic anywhere. That is exactly the failure mode the t-47hc discipline says
    // is invisible on valid input, so it needs its own pin.
    then "well-formed decl wrappers build the same tree" {
        agree_tree("export fn g() -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("/// a doc comment\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("//! a module doc\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("@foo\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("@foo(1, 2)\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        // The stack, in both orders — the recursion means order is observable.
        agree_tree("@foo\nexport fn g() -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_tree("/// doc\n@foo\nexport fn g() -> int { 1 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The TREE half for `enum` — see the F-code block above. A partial enum node (a
    // nameless declaration, a variant list truncated at the first bad variant) has the
    // same F-codes as hand's `(error)` and a different tree, so both halves are needed.
    then "malformed enum recovers to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("enum { A, B }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E { A,\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E { A(x: ) }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E { A( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E< { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E<T { A }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("enum E { 5 }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The TREE half for `type` — see the F-code block above.
    then "malformed type recovers to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("type T =\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type T< = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type T<A = { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type T { x: int }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type T = int without\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("type T = int only { \nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    // The TREE half for the last four keywords — see the F-code block above.
    then "malformed fn / extern / trait / impl recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn a<T() -> int { 1 }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("extern fn () -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("extern fn a(\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("extern a() -> int\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") &&
        agree_bad("trait { fn m() }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("trait T {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("trait T { type }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("impl Foo {\nfn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
        agree_bad("impl Foo { fn m( }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }")
    }
    then "malformed statements + expressions recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = \n return 1\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let = 5\n return 1\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n const C\n return 1\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n if { return 1 }\n return 2\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n while return 2\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n for x { return 3 }\n}") &&
        agree_tree("fn f() -> int {\n match { 1 -> 2 }\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = 1 +\n return x\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let y = (1 + 2\n return y\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let w = foo(1,\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let q = a.\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n return * 3\n}")
    }
    then "multi-error recovery builds the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn a() -> int { 1 + }\nfn b() -> int { 2 }\nthen\nfn c() -> int { * 3 }") &&
        // PINNED for the same reason as its F-code sibling above.
        pin_tree("type T = { x: , y: }\nfn f(a: , b: int) -> int { return a }\nfn g() -> int { let z = ; return z }",
                 "(error)\n(error)\n(return a)\n(fn g () -> int (error)\n(return z))", 6) &&
        agree_bad("fn h() -> int {\n  if { return 1 }\n  while return 2\n  for x { return 3 }\n}")
    }
    then "unclosed-at-EOF constructs recover to the same tree" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n return 1\n") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = (1 + 2 * 3\n") &&
        agree_bad("type T = {\n x: int,\n")
    }
    then "a valid program renders the same NON-error tree under both modes" {
        agree_tree("fn ok() -> int { 1 + 2 }") &&
        rtree(true, "fn ok() -> int { 1 + 2 }") != "<null>" &&
        !rtree(true, "fn ok() -> int { 1 + 2 }").contains("(error)")
    }
}

// The 5c lesson RESOLVED, pinned. A top-level binary-operator-led expression
// statement (`* 5`) was the ONE corpus case where F-codes agreed but the recovery
// TREES diverged: the static generated `parse_expr_stmt` wrapped the FAILED
// expression as `.Ok(Stmt.Expr(Expr.Error))` → `(expr (* (error) 5))`, while hand
// yielded `.Err` → a bare `(error)`. The emit-error fix (emit.av's `ExprStmt`
// build → `Stmt.Error` when `st.had_error`) makes the static parser recover it as
// `(error)` too — closing the exact gap that blocked retiring the interpreter
// re-parse fallback (commit e1a55fef "needs emit-error work"). This spec pins the
// fix so a regression that re-wraps a failed expr-stmt as `(expr (error))` trips.
spec "t-47hc.3: the 5c recovery-TREE divergence is CLOSED (emit-error fix)" {
    then "F-codes agree on the stray top-level `* 5`" {
        static_raw("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }") ==
            exec_oracle("fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }")
    }
    then "the recovery TREES now AGREE: static yields a bare `(error)`, matching hand (not `(expr (*`)" {
        let src = "fn a() -> int { 1 }\n* 5\nfn b() -> int { 2 }"
        agree_bad(src) &&
        rtree(true, src).contains("(error)") &&
        !rtree(true, src).contains("(expr (*") &&
        !rtree(false, src).contains("(expr (*")
    }
}

// ── t-47hc.3.1 — mandatory mid-rule TERMINAL VALIDATION (strict-by-default emit) ──
//
// The generated expression parser now st.reports on a MISSING mandatory mid-rule
// terminal — matching the interpreter's exec_primary/handle_missing (already
// strict) and the hand parser's own consume(...) calls (mod.av:1595-1631):
//   • `is .Variant`  → the `.` and the variant IDENT
//   • `in [ … ]`     → the closing `]`
//   • `in a .. b`    → the `..`
//   • `.Variant(args)` → the variant IDENT and the closing `)`
// Previously the emit lowered these to a bare unvalidated advance, so a malformed
// constructor was silently consumed WITHOUT had_error → the DSL-error→hand fallback
// (parse_expression) never engaged → the recovered TREE could diverge from hand
// even when the F-code matched (CodeRabbit PR #967, Major). With validation, the
// report sets had_error → parse_expression re-parses with the authoritative hand
// oracle, so the F-codes AND the recovered trees match hand byte-for-byte. This
// spec pins that parity over the exact malformed-constructor corpus the ticket
// names (`.`, `.(1)`, `.Some(1`, unclosed `[`, missing `..`).
spec "t-47hc.3.1: malformed mandatory-terminal constructors recover == exec" {
    // These six forms MUST set had_error (a missing mandatory terminal), so assert a
    // NONEMPTY, matching F-code signature — not just parser parity (which an
    // all-clean pair would satisfy vacuously).
    then "malformed enum-ctor / is-check constructors REPORT and agree" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .Some(1\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x is Foo\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x is .\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "malformed in-membership constructors REPORT and agree" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x in [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x in 1 2\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "`.(1)`: strict validation fires internally AND the hand fallback now agrees with it" {
        // `.` then `(` (not IDENT) trips the IDENT validation → had_error → fallback
        // to the hand parser. This block used to assert the fallback parsed `.(1)`
        // CLEANLY — no diagnostic under either parser — and read that as proof the
        // validation "never invents an error the hand parser wouldn't raise."
        //
        // The premise was wrong, and t-6u9b's count oracle is what exposed it: hand
        // was not ACCEPTING `.(1)`, it was SILENTLY DROPPING the `.` and parsing the
        // remaining `(1)` as a group. The grammar's strict validation had been right
        // all along; the oracle it deferred to was the broken one, and because both
        // paths ended up quiet the parity assertion passed. Empty-vs-empty agreement
        // is the one shape a differential oracle cannot interpret.
        //
        // With `parse_primary` fixed to report a `.` it cannot use, the two paths
        // still AGREE — which is what this spec is actually for — but now they agree
        // on REPORTING. The non-invention property is unchanged and better evidenced:
        // it is `agree`/`agree_bad` parity across the whole corpus that establishes it,
        // never a single quiet case.
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .(1)\n return 0\n}") &&
        static_raw("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .(1)\n return 0\n}") != ""
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand (strictly stronger than F-codes)" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .(1)\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .Some(1\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x is Foo\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x is .\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x in [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let b = x in 1 2\n return 0\n}")
    }
}

// t-47hc.4.5: the grammar-native `list_expr` (@try comprehension branch + list branch,
// replacing @hand(list_expr)) recovers from malformed list literals AND comprehensions
// byte-identically to the hand parse_list_expr / parse_list_elements — the flip's error-
// path parity (diff-test only covers VALID programs). A failed comprehension @try rolls
// back so the list branch / enclosing recovery reports; these lock that the recovered
// F-codes AND trees match hand.
spec "t-47hc.4.5: grammar-native list_expr recovers == exec oracle" {
    then "malformed list literals REPORT and agree (unclosed, stray/missing comma)" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1,,2]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1 2]\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "malformed comprehensions REPORT and agree (missing var / collection)" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [a for]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [a for x]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [a for x in]\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand (strictly stronger than F-codes)" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1,,2]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [a for]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [a for x in]\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "well-formed list + comprehension produce NO diagnostics under either mode" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2, 3]\n let w = [x for x in z]\n return 0\n}") &&
        static_raw("fn f() -> int {\n let z = [1, 2, 3]\n let w = [x for x in z]\n return 0\n}") == ""
    }
}

// t-47hc.19: `template_lit` owns the TEMPLATE token natively, and the desugar is now the
// SHARED store-based desugar_template_store both parsers call — so interpolation shape and
// both of its error paths are identical by construction.
//
// NOTE the `dbrace()` indirection: a literal `${` written in THIS file's string would be
// real interpolation of the test's own scope, so the fixture sources are concatenated
// (the scan_string_test precedent).
fn dbrace() -> string { "$" + "{" }

fn tpl_src(body: string) -> string {
    "fn f() -> int {\n let x = 1\n " + body + "\n return 0\n}"
}

spec "t-47hc.19: grammar-native template_lit recovers == exec oracle" {
    then "malformed interpolations REPORT and agree" {
        // unterminated `${x` and an invalid sub-expression `${(}`
        agree_error(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "x\"")) &&
        agree_error(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "(}\""))
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand (strictly stronger than F-codes)" {
        agree_bad(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "x\"")) &&
        agree_bad(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "(}\""))
    }
    then "well-formed templates agree (plain, single, multiple, empty, format specs)" {
        agree(tpl_src("let a = \"plain\"")) &&
        agree(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "x}\"")) &&
        agree(tpl_src("let c = \"" + dbrace() + "x} and " + dbrace() + "x}\"")) &&
        agree(tpl_src("let d = \"\"")) &&
        agree(tpl_src("let e = \"pi=" + dbrace() + "x:.2f} n=" + dbrace() + "x:d}\"")) &&
        static_raw(tpl_src("let b = \"v=" + dbrace() + "x}\"")) == ""
    }
}

// t-47hc.18b: `qualified_expr` (`@pkg::mod::name` + its struct-literal form) is native.
// The `( "::" IDENT )+` carries `@expect("+", …)` so a bare `@name` reports the hand's
// exact wording, not the emitter's generic required-repetition message.
spec "t-47hc.18b: grammar-native qualified_expr recovers == exec oracle" {
    then "malformed qualified references REPORT and agree" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @foo\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @pkg::\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @pkg::T { x: }\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @foo\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @pkg::T { x: }\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "well-formed qualified idents and struct literals agree" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @pkg::mod::name\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let q = @pkg::T { x: 1 }\n return 0\n}")
    }
}

// t-47hc.18: `brace_expr` dispatches natively via `@peek(brace_map)` (the token twin of
// is_map_literal) and `map_literal` is grammar-owned. The hand parse_brace_expr is gone.
spec "t-47hc.18: grammar-native brace_expr / map_literal recover == exec oracle" {
    then "malformed map literals REPORT and agree (missing colon, unclosed, stray comma)" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\": 1, \"b\" 2 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\": 1\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\": , \"b\": 2 }\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\": 1, \"b\" 2 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\": 1\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "well-formed maps, blocks and the map-vs-block split agree" {
        // `{}` is an EMPTY MAP in expression position but a BLOCK in statement position;
        // a `{` with a non-string first token is always a block. Trailing comma allowed.
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let a = { \"k\": 1, \"j\": 2 }\n let b = { \"k\": 1, }\n let c = {}\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let x = 1\n let b = { x + 1 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        // `{ "a" 1 }` is NOT a malformed map: no `:` after the key, so @peek(brace_map)
        // declines and it is a BLOCK of two expression statements — exactly as hand.
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let m = { \"a\" 1 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        static_raw("fn f() -> int {\n let a = { \"k\": 1 }\n let c = {}\n return 0\n}") == ""
    }
}

// t-47hc.17: `with_suffix` is now grammar-native (the hand `parse_with_suffix` is
// DELETED), built on the new FieldInit CapVal seam. The hand's field loop was
// `while !check(RightBrace) { consume_identifier("expected field name") … }`, so its
// trailing `consume(RightBrace, "expected \`}\` after with fields")` was UNREACHABLE —
// the loop exits only when `}` is already current. That is why the grammar reproduces
// it with a FIRST-guarded `( field_init )*` plus `@expect("}", "expected field name")`:
// one message covers BOTH the junk-token case and EOF, exactly as the hand did. These
// cases pin that equivalence — including the follow-on diagnostics, where a divergence
// would show up if the grammar's report-and-continue drifted from the hand's
// report-and-abort.
spec "t-47hc.17: grammar-native with_suffix recovers == exec oracle" {
    then "malformed with-field lists REPORT and agree (junk token, unclosed)" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with { 1 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with { x: 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with x: 2 }\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "the recovered TREES agree with hand (strictly stronger than F-codes)" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with { 1 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with { x: 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1 }\n let b = a with x: 2 }\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "well-formed `with` — including SHORTHAND, comma-less and CHAINED — agrees" {
        // The hand loop needs no comma between fields (`{ x y }` is two shorthand
        // inits), and `mk_field_init` must expand a bare `name` to `name: Ident(name)`.
        // Chaining exercises the span contract: rebind_with reads each partial With's
        // `.hi`, so a wrong sp_start would corrupt the second suffix's span.
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1, y: 2 }\n let b = a with { x: 10 } with { y: 20 }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1, y: 2 }\n let x = 5\n let b = a with { x }\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1, y: 2 }\n let x = 5\n let y = 6\n let b = a with { x y }\n return 0\n}") &&
        static_raw("fn f() -> int {\n let a = P { x: 1, y: 2 }\n let b = a with { x: 10 } with { y: 20 }\n return 0\n}") == ""
    }
}

// t-47hc.16: `paren_expr` now replaces the former @hand leaf in the generated
// expression parser. Unlike valid-input differential tests, this oracle makes
// a failed @try/tuple/lambda parse observable: the generated parser must fall
// through to the same hand recovery tree and diagnostics as Parser A.
spec "t-47hc.16: grammar-native paren_expr recovers == exec oracle" {
    then "unclosed grouping REPORTS exactly like hand" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1 + 2\n return a\n}")
    }
    then "unclosed tuple REPORTS exactly like hand" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let pair = (1, 2\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "untyped and typed lambda failures REPORT exactly like hand" {
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x -> x\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x) ->\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x: ) -> x\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_error("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x: int) ->\n return 0\n}")
    }
    // t-oejs — NON-IDENT bases, deliberately. The ident and literal primaries take
    // DIFFERENT paths into `paren_expr`, and an ident-only corpus let a real divergence
    // hide: `(x) ->` agreed only because both sides were wrong the same way, while
    // `(1) ->` (a native token base) diverged unnoticed. Every malformed paren form is
    // now probed with a literal base as well as an ident one.
    then "a missing lambda body keeps the binding rather than erasing it" {
        agree_bad("let a = (1) ->") &&
        agree_bad("let a = (x) ->") &&
        agree_bad("let a = (1, 2) ->") &&
        // The committed forms must be UNAFFECTED — a valid lambda still wins over the
        // grouping fallback at every arity, typed and untyped.
        agree_tree("let f = (x) -> x + 1") &&
        agree_tree("let g = (a, b) -> a") &&
        agree_tree("let h = (x: int) -> x") &&
        agree_tree("let e = () -> 1") &&
        agree_tree("let t = (1, 2)")
    }
    then "every malformed paren form recovers to the identical tree" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1 + 2\n return a\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let pair = (1, 2\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x -> x\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x) ->\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x: ) -> x\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let id = (x: int) ->\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "well-formed group tuple and lambda forms produce no diagnostics" {
        agree("fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1 + 2)\n let pair = (1, 2)\n let typed = (x: int) -> x\n return a\n}") &&
        static_raw("fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1 + 2)\n let pair = (1, 2)\n let typed = (x: int) -> x\n return a\n}") == ""
    }
}

// ── t-6u9b — SILENT TOKEN LOSS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The invariant: no token reaching the parser is ever discarded without either
// being consumed by a successful parse or producing a diagnostic. Silent loss is
// worse than a bad recovery tree — the user gets NO error for text the compiler
// threw away, and the program compiles as though they had never written it.
//
// The ticket was filed on a stray `->` inside a fn body, which reported nothing
// under the hand path while the static path reported it. That instance is fixed
// (t-zrn3's exec_node tail + the ExprStmt `had_error` check made the executor
// recover a stray statement-position token as a bare `Stmt.Error`), and the first
// spec below pins it so it cannot come back.
//
// The CLASS was not fixed, and the ticket's real lesson is how it stayed hidden:
// every oracle in this file compares the two parsers to EACH OTHER. Applying the
// new `agree_bad` — same trees AND both parsers actually reporting — across the
// whole corpus immediately surfaced a live second instance the tree oracle had
// been green on all along, pinned by the second spec below.
spec "t-6u9b: no token is discarded without a diagnostic" {
    // The ticket's own case, verbatim. `let a = (1) ->` leaves the `->` behind once
    // t-oejs stops erasing the binding; the statement loop must REPORT it, not step
    // over it. Counted, not just shaped: a silent drop and a clean recovery differ
    // only in the diagnostic, and only on one of the two paths.
    then "a stray `->` left inside a fn body is reported on BOTH paths" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1) ->\n return 0\n}") &&
        rdiags(false, "fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1) ->\n return 0\n}") > 0 &&
        rdiags(true, "fn f() -> int {\n let a = (1) ->\n return 0\n}") > 0
    }
    // The second instance, found by the count oracle. `parse_primary` consumed the
    // `.` for the contextual enum-variant shorthand (`.Variant` / `.Variant(args)`),
    // found no identifier after it, and fell through to the rest of the dispatch —
    // with the `.` already eaten. So `.(1)` parsed as the group `(1)`, `.[1]` as a
    // list, `."s"` as a string: whatever followed simply absorbed the position and
    // the `.` was gone. Zero diagnostics, on BOTH paths, which is why `agree_tree`
    // never saw it. In primary position a `.` can only open the variant shorthand
    // (a postfix `.field` needs an operand to its left and is `dot_is_postfix`'s
    // job; `..` lexes as its own token), so a non-identifier after it is an error.
    then "a `.` not followed by a variant name is reported, not swallowed" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .(1)\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .[1]\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .\"s\"\n return 0\n}") &&
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let x = .5\n return 0\n}")
    }
    // The token must not merely be reported — it must not be silently ABSORBED into
    // a well-formed-looking node either. `.(1)` recovering as `(group 1)` would be
    // the same bug wearing a diagnostic: the tree would still claim the user wrote
    // something they did not.
    then "the swallowed form recovers to `(error)`, exactly like a bare trailing `.`" {
        let dotted = rtree(false, "fn f() -> int {\n let x = .(1)\n return 0\n}")
        let plain = rtree(false, "fn f() -> int {\n let x = .\n return 0\n}")
        dotted == plain && dotted.contains("(error)") && !dotted.contains("(group 1)")
    }
    // The shorthand itself is untouched — the fix rejects only the forms that had no
    // reading, so every valid `.Variant` shape still parses with no diagnostic.
    then "the valid variant shorthand still parses at every shape" {
        rdiags(false, "fn f() -> E {\n return .A\n}") == 0 &&
        rdiags(false, "fn f() -> E {\n return .Some(1)\n}") == 0 &&
        rdiags(false, "fn f() -> E {\n return .Pair(1, 2)\n}") == 0 &&
        rdiags(true, "fn f() -> E {\n return .Some(1)\n}") == 0
    }
}

// t-47hc.33.1 — a field name that is a RESERVED-but-inactive keyword.
//
// `consume_field_name` accepts `Tk.KwReserved` (`ref` `owned` `move` `borrow` …) as well
// as an identifier, so `s.ref` PARSES and fails later in typeck as `no field \`ref\``.
// That is a semantic error, not a syntactic one, and the distinction is the whole point:
// if a front end rejected `s.ref` at PARSE time it would report a syntax F-code instead,
// and the user would see a different error for the same program.
//
// The parse is unreachable in a *valid* program — a field named `ref` cannot be DECLARED
// (`type S = { ref: int }` is F0001, "reserved keyword ... cannot be used as an
// identifier"), so no type ever has one. That is exactly why this belongs here rather
// than in the diff-test corpus: the corpus needs programs that COMPILE, and none of these
// do. IR equality can never cover this path; F-code agreement is the only oracle for it.
//
// These pass today because `@hand(postfix_suffix)` still catches what the native branches
// decline. They are here for t-47hc.33.2, which deletes that delegate: at that moment the
// native `.`-led branches become the only path, and without the FIELDNAME terminal from
// t-47hc.33.1 every one of these flips from a typeck F-code to a parse F-code. Deleting
// the delegate with this spec red is the regression it exists to stop.
spec "t-47hc.33.1: a reserved-keyword field name parses on every path" {
    then "`.ref` reports the SEMANTIC error, identically on all three paths" {
        agree_all("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s.ref))\n}")
    }
    then "and so do the other reserved spellings" {
        agree_all("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s.owned))\n}") &&
        agree_all("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s.move))\n}")
    }
    then "chained past a reserved field name" {
        agree_all("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s.ref.a))\n}")
    }
    then "the optional-chain form agrees too" {
        agree_all("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s?.borrow))\n}")
    }
    then "an ACTIVE keyword after the dot is a PARSE error — and still agrees" {
        // The other half of the FIELDNAME contract. `if` is Tk.KwIf, not KwReserved, so
        // both front ends must REJECT it; a branch matching the DSL's coarse `KW` kind
        // would have accepted it and diverged here.
        agree_error("type S = { a: int }\nfn main() {\n let s = S { a: 1 }\n println(string(s.if))\n}")
    }
}


/// Tree equality across ALL THREE implementations, for VALID input. The `agree_*`
/// family above all key on DIAGNOSTICS, which a valid program has none of — so they
/// pass vacuously here and cannot say anything about what a valid program PARSES
/// INTO. This is the valid-input twin: same three paths, compared on the tree.
fn tree_agrees_everywhere(source: string) -> bool {
    // TWO legs. This had three while a hand row existed; t-47hc.4.1 rewrote that row to
    // "exec" and left the binding in place, so it called `rtree_at("exec", …)` twice and
    // one of the two comparisons was a value against itself. Raised in review on PR
    // #1220 — the same shape this file exists to police, arriving by rename rather than
    // by design. The name is kept: it still means "on every engine there is".
    let e = rtree_at("emit", source)
    let x = rtree_at("exec", source)
    if e != x {
        println("TREE mismatch across engines")
        println(source)
        println("emit=" + e)
        println("exec=" + x)
    }
    e == x
}

// ── t-47hc.26: STATEMENT-position `if let`, which every other oracle is blind to ──
//
// These are VALID programs, and that is exactly why the divergence survived: the whole
// `agree_*` family compares DIAGNOSTICS, a valid program has none, so all four of these
// passed VACUOUSLY here for as long as the corpus has held them. `agree_all` on the
// nested case (the spec at the top of this file) was green throughout. Only the TREE
// distinguishes the two lowerings, and nothing rendered these trees.
//
// What it was hiding: `iflet_stmt` routed statement-position `if let` to `expression`,
// so it lowered through `build_if_let_EXPR` — an `ExprStmt` holding a block-EXPRESSION
// holding an `if-expr` — where the hand's `parse_if_let_statement` builds a `Stmt.Block`
// holding a `Stmt.If`. Both run, which is why the compiler worked; they are not the same
// tree. The sharpest case is the third, with no `else`: an if-EXPRESSION must have a
// value, so the expression lowering SYNTHESISES an empty else block, and
// `if let v = x { t = v }` acquired an else arm the source never wrote.
//
// Note these diverged on emit AND exec in lockstep — a NATIVE-vs-hand gap, not the
// executor-only class of t-zrn3. Both native engines share one grammar rule, so both
// inherited one wrong lowering, and a two-path comparison (emit vs exec) would have
// called it agreement. The hand is the oracle precisely for this.
//
// Fixed by giving `iflet_stmt` its own rule lowering through the SHARED
// `build_if_let_stmt` (core/arena.av) that the hand already calls.
spec "t-47hc.26: statement-position `if let` lowers to the hand's STATEMENT tree" {
    then "a statement-position `if let` is a Stmt.Block + Stmt.If, not an ExprStmt" {
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n mut t = 0\n if let v = x { t = v } else { return 0 - 1 }\n t\n}") &&
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn g(x: int?) -> int {\n if let v = x { return v } else { return 0 }\n}")
    }
    then "with NO else, the else stays ABSENT — no synthesized empty block" {
        // The regression that motivated the shared-core routing. An if-EXPRESSION needs
        // a value and so must invent an else; `Stmt.If` takes `none`, and the hand
        // passes `none`. Asserting the tree is the only way to see the difference.
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n mut t = 0\n if let v = x { t = v }\n t\n}")
    }
    then "a nested if-let picks its position independently, and mints in hand order" {
        // The outer is EXPRESSION position (a `let` initializer) and the inner is
        // STATEMENT position inside the then-block, so one source line exercises both
        // lowerings at once. Also pins the `@gensym(iflet)` order: the outer must be
        // `$iflet$1` and the inner `$iflet$2`, which is why the mint sits between `"if"`
        // and `"let"` in BOTH rules rather than at build time.
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(a: int?, b: int?) -> int {\n let r = if let p = a { if let q = b { q } else { p } } else { 0 }\n r\n}")
    }
}

// ── t-47hc.33.2: the `!` compounds, asserted instead of asserted-about ──────────
//
// Deleting `@hand(postfix_suffix)` rested on a claim that was MEASURED and then written
// down only in prose (postfix_flip_test): "measured with the delegate actually deleted,
// every compound agrees: `x!\n?`, `x!\n.f`, `x!\n[0]`, `x!\n(1)`, `x!!`, `x!?`". That
// was the last stated blocker on the deletion, cleared by a measurement no spec kept.
//
// A measurement that is not a spec decays into folklore, and this one gates a DELETED
// safety net. So the compounds are corpus now — and asserting them found the claim to be
// FALSE for one of the six. See the divergence note below `x!\n?` is deliberately absent
// from, in the same shape this file already uses for `a?.`.
//
// They are VALID programs, which is why they need their own assertion: the whole
// `agree_*` family compares DIAGNOSTICS, and a valid program has none, so every one of
// them passes vacuously here. Only the TREE distinguishes `x!\n[0]` read as one indexed
// chain from `x!` followed by a fresh list-literal statement.
spec "t-47hc.33.2: the `!` compounds parse identically on all three paths" {
    then "a NEXT-LINE suffix after `!` does not fold onto the previous line" {
        // Newline-sensitivity is the whole question. `[0]` and `(1)` must start FRESH
        // statements (a list literal and a group), not become an index and a call —
        // which is what the entry guard's same-line conjunct is for.
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n [0]\n return 0\n}") &&
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n (1)\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "a next-line `.field` DOES fold — and that is the designed asymmetry" {
        // `.` is the one postfix whose entry guard requires same-line ONLY under
        // `cell_mode`, because cross-line method chaining is idiomatic. Pinned so a
        // later "consistency" tidy-up cannot quietly make `.` newline-sensitive too.
        tree_agrees_everywhere("type S = { f: int }\nfn f(x: S?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n .f\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "and the SAME-LINE compounds, which the hand ran as ONE step" {
        // `x!!` and `x!?` are the `!` loop's own shape — the hand consumed the run and
        // then dispatched without re-guarding. Native, each is a separate re-entered
        // step; that this makes no observable difference is the claim under test.
        //
        // The doubly-nullable parameter goes through an ALIAS. Written inline as
        // `x: int??` the fixture does not parse AT ALL — `??` in a parameter type is
        // `error[F0001]: expected \`)\` after parameters`, on every path — so the
        // assertion agreed about recovery from a malformed PARAMETER LIST and never
        // reached the `!!` it names. Green, and measuring nothing: the same
        // typo'd-fixture trap this file records retiring BARE_IDENT_COMP_INST for.
        //
        // `exec_oracle(src) == ""` is what makes the repair hold. `tree_agrees_everywhere`
        // compares RENDERED TREES and nothing else, so it cannot reject a parse error the
        // three paths share — which is exactly how the `int??` version passed. Verifying
        // the replacement compiles and then writing "verified" in a comment would repeat
        // the defect one level up, so the clean parse is ASSERTED. Same idiom the
        // same-line control below uses. (`agree_all` is deliberately not added: on a clean
        // program it compares empty against empty, the vacuity this file polices.)
        let ubang = "type W = int?\nfn f(x: W?) -> int {\n let a = x!!\n return 0\n}"
        tree_agrees_everywhere(ubang) && exec_oracle(ubang) == "" &&
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!?\n return 0\n}")
    }
    then "a next-line `?` after a `!` run does NOT fold — the fallthrough re-guards" {
        // t-47hc.33.2 — this case used to DIVERGE and was carried as a comment rather
        // than an assertion. Measured then:
        //
        //     hand  error[F0040]: expected expression
        //     emit  <clean>   — folded to (let a (? (call __avra_force_unwrap (x))))
        //     exec  <clean>   — same as emit
        //     prod  <clean>
        //
        // Mechanism: `postfix_step` (parse/mod.av) consumes a run of `!` in a `while`
        // loop and then re-dispatched on `if self.check(Tk.Question)` with NO re-test of
        // the entry guard's `allow_dot_postfix && current_line == prev_line`, so a `?` on
        // the FOLLOWING line folded into a Try that the entry guard would have rejected.
        // The fallthrough now re-tests those conjuncts: a guard belongs on every dispatch
        // of a form, not only the first. All four paths report F0040 together.
        //
        // This is a STRICTNESS change — the parser now rejects what it accepted — so it
        // ships under `intended-strictness-change`, not as a silent correction.
        //
        // Its siblings never diverged, which is why this was one bug and not a class:
        // `[` and `(` reach the same fallthrough but land in a fresh step on every path,
        // and `.` is legal cross-line by design (above).
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n ?\n return 0\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n ?\n return 0\n}") &&
            // The exact diagnostic, not merely agreement — an equal-and-nonzero
            // signature is not evidence of the RIGHT rejection.
            exec_oracle("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!\n ?\n return 0\n}").contains("F0040")
    }
    then "the SAME-LINE `!` compounds still fold — the guard did not over-reject" {
        // The control for the strictness change above: `x!?` on ONE line must stay
        // clean on every path. A re-guard that also killed this would be a regression,
        // and nothing else in this spec would have caught it.
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!?\n return 0\n}") &&
            exec_oracle("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n let a = x!?\n return 0\n}") == ""
    }
}

fn agree_pos(source: string) -> bool {
    let h = poslist(false, source)
    let n = poslist(true, source)
    if h != n {
        println("SrcPos side-table divergence")
        println(source)
        println("exec  =" + h)
        println("emit  =" + n)
    }
    h == n
}

/// PINNED positions — the shipping parser's exact stamped `line:col` sequence.
///
/// The component-instantiation corpus below cannot be a differential: the executor
/// does not answer these forms at all (it stamps the trailing `fn` and nothing else),
/// so `agree_pos` on them would be asserting that one engine matches another engine's
/// refusal. Pinning is what the spec actually wanted anyway — its stated job is to
/// catch a DROPPED or MISPLACED `record_pos`, and a literal expected sequence catches
/// that directly, without depending on a second implementation having an opinion.
fn pin_pos(source: string, expected: string) -> bool {
    let n = poslist(true, source)
    if n != expected {
        println("PINNED SrcPos sequence moved")
        println(source)
        println("expected=" + expected)
        println("actual  =" + n)
    }
    n == expected
}

// Landed BEFORE slice B2b routed the four component-body arms, for the same reason
// the invalid corpus was: the guard has to exist before the change it guards. It
// EARNED that on the flip — B2b went red here (and on the tree oracle) the moment
// the arms became reachable, and green again only once each reproduced
// `push_body_elem`'s stamping. It remains the only spec in the repo that can catch a
// dropped or misplaced `record_pos`, because positions reach neither the IR (so
// diff-test is blind) nor the tree shape.
spec "t-47hc.5.1: the per-statement SrcPos side-table is stamped exactly" {
    // Each expectation is the sequence `push_body_elem` produces: body rows first, in
    // source order, then the enclosing block, then the trailing `fn`. A rule that
    // stopped stamping loses its entry; one that stamped at the wrong token moves it.
    then "a native instantiation body stamps every body statement" {
        pin_pos("component C inst { key \"v\"\n  let z = 1\n  foo()\n}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }",
                "2:3 3:3 1:1 5:17 ")
    }
    then "config pairs do not disturb the body's positions" {
        pin_pos("component C inst { a 1\n  b \"s\"\n  let z = 1\n}\nfn f() -> int { 1 }",
                "3:3 1:1 5:17 ")
    }
    // The four arms B2b made native (nested `component`, `ident name {`, `ident {`,
    // `ident() {`). Their stamping did not move when they flipped — which is the
    // assertion, and it failed until each routed arm stamped like `push_body_elem`.
    then "the once-declined arms are stamped at their own lead token" {
        pin_pos("component C inst { nested name { let z = 1 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:34 1:20 1:1 2:17 ") &&
        pin_pos("component C inst { nested { let z = 1 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:29 1:20 1:1 2:17 ") &&
        pin_pos("component C inst { run() { let z = 1 } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:28 1:20 1:1 2:17 ") &&
        pin_pos("component C inst { component D e { } }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:20 1:1 2:17 ")
    }
    then "positions survive a MALFORMED body too (recovery still stamps)" {
        pin_pos("component C inst { let x }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:1 2:17 ") &&
        pin_pos("component C inst { nested name }\nfn f() -> int { 1 }", "1:1 2:17 ")
    }
}

// WHY: t-47hc.5.1 slice C — the BARE-IDENT component instantiation
// (`acct_sr a { … }`, no `component` keyword). Landed BEFORE the routing, per the
// standing discipline: these must go RED if the form is routed without reproducing
// the hand's abort behaviour, and they are the only witness that can — routing is
// invisible on VALID input by construction, because the native rule and the hand
// agree there.
//
// One case per abort path in `parse_component_block_bare`:
//   - instance name missing        -> the hand FALLS BACK to parse_statement
//   - `{` missing after instance   -> "expected `{` after instance name"
//   - body brace never closed      -> "expected `}` at end of component block"
//   - malformed config pair        -> parse_comp_config_pairs reports
//   - malformed body statement     -> parse_comp_block_body reports
//
// Each needs a real `component` DEFINITION first: the bare form is dispatched from
// the feature REGISTRY (register_component_name), so an unregistered ident is an
// ordinary expression and would measure nothing.
fn CDEF() -> string {
    "component acct_sr {\n let balance: int = 100\n}\n"
}

spec "t-47hc.5.1 slice C: bare-IDENT component instantiation recovers as the hand does" {
    // `acct_sr a` with no `{` is NOT malformed — it parses as two ordinary identifier
    // expression statements on both paths, with no diagnostic. It looked like an abort
    // path from reading parse_component_block_bare; measuring it showed otherwise, so
    // it is a TREE agreement rather than an agree_bad case.
    then "a registered name + instance with no brace is two expressions, not an error" {
        agree_all(CDEF() + "fn u() -> int {\n acct_sr a\n 0\n}")
    }

    then "and on ALL THREE parser paths, including the executor" {
        agree_all(CDEF() + "fn u() -> int {\n acct_sr a\n 0\n}") &&
        agree_all(CDEF() + "fn u() -> int {\n acct_sr a { balance = 1\n 0\n}")
    }

    // NOT included, and deliberately so: `acct_sr a { balance = }` (a config pair with
    // no value) diverges between the static path and the hand TODAY — measured before
    // any slice C routing, on a form the hand still owns. It is a PRE-EXISTING gap in
    // component-body recovery that this corpus exposed, not something routing caused,
    // and it wants its own ticket rather than a red baseline here. The unterminated
    // sibling (`{ balance = 1`) DOES agree on all three paths and is asserted above,
    // so the malformed-body class is still guarded.

    // The instance-name-missing case is NOT an error: the hand falls back to
    // parse_statement, so `acct_sr` alone is an ordinary identifier expression.
    // Pinned as a TREE agreement rather than agree_bad, since nothing reports.
    then "a registered name with no instance falls back to an expression, as the hand does" {
        agree_tree(CDEF() + "fn u() -> int {\n acct_sr\n 0\n}")
    }
}

// WHY: t-myz7 — statement-position `if let` routes through `expr_stmt` now, and the
// hand desugars it with `build_if_let_stmt` (a Stmt.Block) where the expression path
// uses `build_if_let_expr` (a Stmt.Expr). Raised in review as possibly changing the
// AST or rejecting statement-only else arms.
//
// The two desugars ARE structurally different, so this is not idle: both build
// `let tmp = subject` plus an if, but one yields a StmtId and the other an ExprId.
// Measured, they lower identically — hand and production emit byte-identical IR for
// both shapes below, including the `return`-in-else case. `expression` reaches
// `if_expr`, whose then/else are `block_expr`, so statement-only arms parse fine.
//
// Pinned here because the existing t-47hc.26 if-let cases are all EXPRESSION position
// (`let r = if let v = x { 1 }`), so nothing covered the statement form's tree.
spec "t-myz7: statement-position `if let` agrees with the hand on the TREE" {
    then "the plain statement form agrees" {
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n mut t = 0\n if let v = x { t = v }\n t\n}")
    }

    then "a statement-only `else` arm agrees — `return` inside the else" {
        agree_all("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n mut t = 0\n if let v = x { t = v } else { return 0 - 1 }\n t\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn g(x: int?) -> int {\n if let v = x { return v } else { return 0 }\n}")
    }

    then "the malformed statement forms recover identically" {
        agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n if let = x { 1 }\n 0\n}") &&
            agree_bad("fn f(x: int?) -> int {\n if let v x { 1 }\n 0\n}")
    }
}

// WHY: t-myz7 — an ANNOTATED or DOC-COMMENTED component declaration. `component_decl`
// was missing from `wrapped_inner`, the shared inner set of both `annot_decl` and
// `doc_decl`, so `@expand(expand_cli) component cli {}` had no native path and fell
// to `@hand(decl_hand)`. That was 157 sites in packages/cli/src/main.av — the LAST
// form still holding the delegate.
//
// Not `@expand`-specific: a plain `@inline` on a component refused identically, the
// bare `component cli {}` was already native, and `@expand` on a `fn` was fine. Only
// the annotation-plus-component combination reached the gap.
spec "t-myz7: an annotated / doc-commented component agrees with the hand" {
    // `tree_agrees_everywhere`, NOT `agree_all`, for the valid forms: `agree_all`
    // compares errcode signatures, which on VALID input is empty-vs-empty and would
    // pass even if the three paths built DIFFERENT trees. Raised in review, and
    // correct — the whole point of this spec is the tree the new `wrapped_inner`
    // branch produces.
    then "the valid annotated and doc-commented forms build the same TREE everywhere" {
        tree_agrees_everywhere("@expand(expand_cli)\ncomponent cli {}") &&
        tree_agrees_everywhere("@inline\ncomponent cli {}") &&
        tree_agrees_everywhere("/// docs\ncomponent cli {}") &&
        agree_all("@expand(expand_cli)\ncomponent cli {}") &&
        agree_all("@inline\ncomponent cli {}") &&
        agree_all("/// docs\ncomponent cli {}")
    }

    then "an annotation with no declaration after it still reports, identically" {
        agree_bad("@expand(expand_cli)\n") &&
            agree_bad("@inline\n") &&
            agree_all("@inline\n") &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere("@inline\n")
    }

    then "a malformed annotated component recovers the same way" {
        agree_bad("@inline\ncomponent {}") &&
            agree_bad("@inline\ncomponent cli {") &&
            agree_all("@inline\ncomponent {}") &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere("@inline\ncomponent {}")
    }

    // `doc_decl` shares the changed `wrapped_inner`, so its malformed forms need the
    // same coverage as the annotation ones — also raised in review.
    then "a malformed DOC-commented component recovers the same way" {
        agree_bad("/// docs\ncomponent {}") &&
            agree_bad("/// docs\ncomponent cli {") &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere("/// docs\ncomponent {}")
    }
}


// ── `primary_base`'s residue — the corpus, landed BEFORE the routing (t-myz7) ──
//
// Corpus-first, per the routing discipline: these pass TODAY, because the hand owns
// them. That is the correct baseline. The demonstration that counts comes at the
// deletion — each must go RED if `@hand(primary_base)` is removed without the native
// path reproducing what the delegate was providing.
//
// WHY THESE FORMS. `primary_base` measured ZERO across all 1005 corpus files when
// refused BY NAME, which reads like a dead leaf and is not one. A `primary_base`
// refusal rolls the enclosing declaration `@try` back into `decl_hand`, which then
// serves the item silently — so a per-leaf zero only ever proves "nothing needs THIS
// leaf that the other cannot also serve". Refusing BOTH (`*`) and attributing each
// refusal to the file under test tells the truth: 96 refusals across 10 of the 249
// non-test compiler sources, 18 of them in `avra_grammar.av` alone. Minimised, the
// residue is two things, and both are pinned below:
//
//   grammar { r = "a" }   reaches primary_base   <- the FEATURE-FORM seam
//   table { }             reaches primary_base   <- same seam
//   let v = )             reaches primary_base   <- the F0040 error path
//   fn f() -> int { 1 }   reaches nothing        <- control
//
// This is a REDISCOVERY, not news: t-47hc.5 recorded on 2026-08-01 that "the
// `table { … }`/`grammar { … }` feature forms still need the seam". It is pinned here
// because nothing in the suite asserted it, and because the by-name measurement that
// hid it is the one a future slice would naturally reach for again.
//
// Every fixture below was checked to PARSE before being used (`parse_errs=0` for the
// valid ones, exactly one `F0040` for the malformed one) — the discipline this file
// exists to enforce, after an anchor sat here malformed and green for months.
spec "t-myz7: `primary_base`'s residue — the feature-form seam and the F0040 path" {
    then "a `grammar { … }` literal builds the same TREE on all three paths" {
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn g() -> int {\n let gp = grammar {\n  r = \"a\"\n }\n 0\n}") &&
            // …and every path agrees on the DIAGNOSTIC signature, with no parse-phase
            // code in it. Without this the tree assertion alone would accept a fixture
            // that never parsed — see `sig_has_parse_code`.
            agree_all("fn g() -> int {\n let gp = grammar {\n  r = \"a\"\n }\n 0\n}") &&
            !sig_has_parse_code(exec_oracle("fn g() -> int {\n let gp = grammar {\n  r = \"a\"\n }\n 0\n}"))
    }

    then "a `table { … }` literal builds the same TREE on all three paths" {
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { }\n 0\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { }\n 0\n}") &&
            // This one is genuinely diagnostic-FREE, so pin that exactly rather than
            // settling for the weaker no-parse-code bar the `grammar` case needs.
            exec_oracle("fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { }\n 0\n}") == ""
    }

    // The delegate's OTHER job, and the one the `@hand(postfix_suffix)` retirement
    // proves is easy to miss: owning a DIAGNOSTIC. `parse_leaf_common(false)` handles
    // only the splice and the prefix operators — which the grammar's own `unary` /
    // `@when(at_quote_top)` branches already own — so everything else is
    // `parse_primary`, whose residue is `error[F0040]: expected expression` for a token
    // that starts no expression. Deleting the leaf without an `@fallback` carrying that
    // message would leave nobody reporting it, exactly as deleting `postfix_suffix`
    // once left the trailing-dot `expected field name` unowned.
    then "a token that starts NO expression reports EXACTLY F0040, and recovers identically" {
        agree_bad("fn f() -> int {\n let v = )\n 0\n}") &&
            agree_all("fn f() -> int {\n let v = )\n 0\n}") &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f() -> int {\n let v = )\n 0\n}") &&
            // The exact signature, not merely "some equal nonzero set". Raised in
            // review and correct: `agree_bad` requires only a nonzero EQUAL count and
            // `agree_all` only an equal LIST, so all three paths emitting some other
            // code — or emitting F0040 twice — would pass. This spec's whole subject is
            // the F0040 path, so it asserts F0040, once, on the oracle.
            exec_oracle("fn f() -> int {\n let v = )\n 0\n}") == "F0040 "
    }

    // The control. Without it the three assertions above could all be satisfied by a
    // harness that agrees with itself everywhere, which is this file's standing
    // vacuity failure mode.
    then "a plain function — the control — agrees, and is diagnostic-free" {
        tree_agrees_everywhere("fn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            agree_all("fn f() -> int { 1 }") &&
            exec_oracle("fn f() -> int { 1 }") == ""
    }
}

// ── the `table { … }` literal — the corpus, landed BEFORE the routing (t-myz7) ──
//
// The LAST valid input in the compiler that reaches a production `@hand` leaf. With
// `grammar { … }` native (#1159), refusing BOTH leaves attributes 17 refusals to exactly
// one file — `test_runner/mod.av` — and all of them are its two `table<…> { … }`
// literals. Nothing else in 249 non-test sources needs either leaf.
//
// Corpus-first, per the routing discipline: these pass TODAY because the hand owns them.
// The demonstration that counts comes at the flip — each must go RED if `table_lit` is
// routed without reproducing what `parse_table_literal` provides.
//
// ONE CASE PER ABORT PATH of parse_table_literal (parse/mod.av:1284), because the three
// SEMANTIC aborts are the part a native rule is most likely to get subtly wrong — they
// are checks over collected lists, not parse structure:
//
//   header runs to EOF                  the `{`-unterminated path
//   row narrower than the header        "table row has N cell(s) but the header declares M"
//   row wider than the header           the same message, other direction
//   duplicate column name               "duplicate table column `a`"
//   typed `table<Row> { … }`            the `<` WORD `>` `{` entry, valid
//   nested table in a cell              exercises cell_mode SAVE/RESTORE
//
// WHY `msgs_agree_bad` AND NOT `agree_bad` HERE. Measured before writing the spec: all
// three malformed shapes produce the IDENTICAL F-code signature `F0001 F0013` — the
// semantic message rides on F0001 and the recovery cascade adds F0013 every time. So a
// code-level oracle cannot distinguish "reported the duplicate-column error" from
// "reported the wrong one of the three" from "reported a generic alternation failure".
// Only the message discriminates, which is why this block asserts on messages and the
// helper exists at all.
spec "t-myz7: the `table { … }` literal recovers == hand on every abort path" {
    then "a header that runs to EOF reports identically on all three paths" {
        // EOF while the HEADER IS STILL OPEN. The earlier form of this fixture ended
        // `…table { a\n 0\n}` — that lone `}` CLOSES THE TABLE and leaves the `fn` body
        // unclosed, so it measured fn-body EOF recovery and never reached
        // `parse_table_literal`'s unterminated exit at all. Measured, the difference is
        // the whole point of the case:
        //
        //   …table { a\n 0\n}   error[F0001]: expected `}` after function body
        //   …table { a          error[F0001]: expected `}` to close table   ← this path
        //                       error[F0001]: expected `}` after function body
        //
        // Both agree on all three paths, which is exactly why the wrong one stayed green.
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { a"
        msgs_agree_bad(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere(src)
    }

    then "a row NARROWER than the header names both widths" {
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { a | b\n  1\n }\n 0\n}"
        msgs_agree_bad(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere(src) &&
            // The exact message, not merely "some equal set" — this is the assertion the
            // F-code signature cannot make.
            errmsgs(mode_env("exec"), src).contains("table row has 1 cell(s) but the header declares 2 column(s)")
    }

    then "a row WIDER than the header names both widths" {
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { a\n  1 | 2\n }\n 0\n}"
        msgs_agree_bad(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere(src) &&
            errmsgs(mode_env("exec"), src).contains("table row has 2 cell(s) but the header declares 1 column(s)")
    }

    then "a duplicate column name is rejected, naming the column" {
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table { a | a\n  1 | 2\n }\n 0\n}"
        msgs_agree_bad(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            tree_agrees_everywhere(src) &&
            errmsgs(mode_env("exec"), src).contains("duplicate table column `a`")
    }

    // The two VALID shapes. They belong in this corpus because the flip's risk is not
    // only "fails to report" — it is equally "reports where the hand accepts", and a
    // rule gated on the wrong entry test would reject exactly these.
    then "a TYPED `table<Row> { … }` parses clean on all three paths" {
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table<Row> { a\n  1\n }\n 0\n}"
        tree_agrees_everywhere(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            exec_oracle(src) == ""
    }

    // cell_mode is SAVED and RESTORED around the row loop precisely so a table inside a
    // cell leaves the outer table's mode intact. Nothing asserted it before.
    //
    // BOTH tables carry a row type deliberately. The first draft used bare `table { … }`
    // and failed the diagnostic-free bar — measured, the cause is TYPECK, not parsing:
    // an untyped table with columns is `F1042 table literal has no row type`, once per
    // table. The fixture parsed perfectly. Ascribing the row types keeps the assertion
    // at the strict `== ""` bar instead of weakening it to `!sig_has_parse_code`, so a
    // future parse-phase regression here cannot hide behind a tolerated diagnostic.
    then "a row's LAST cell does not swallow the next row's leading sign" {
        // `@on(cell_mode)` is a PER-ITEM marker, so the repeated cell needs its own —
        // the hand sets `self.cell_mode = true` around the WHOLE row loop. With it on
        // only the first cell, cells 2..N parse with cell_mode OFF, and
        // `parse_binary_pratt_g_id` refuses a cross-line `+`/`-` ONLY while cell_mode is
        // set, so `2` at the end of a row could take the next row's `-3` as a binary
        // operand and silently merge two rows into one.
        //
        // THE NOTE THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS RETRACTED, and worth keeping so the
        // retraction is legible. It read: "this case passes both before and after that
        // fix — I could not construct an input where the missing marker actually
        // diverged … it is NOT a fail-before/pass-after witness". That was true of the
        // engines it was measured against (emit and the hand), and false of the one it
        // was not: the EXECUTOR had no cell_mode guard on its binary ladder at all, so
        // this exact input folded `2 - 3` into one cell and reported "row has 3 cell(s)
        // but the header declares 2 column(s)". It became a fail-before/pass-after
        // witness the moment t-47hc.4.1 pointed this corpus at emit-vs-exec, and the fix
        // is `cell_mode_blocks_binary` in executor.av. The lesson generalises: "I could
        // not construct a diverging input" is a statement about the oracles in reach,
        // never about the property.
        let src = "type R2 = { a: int, b: int }\nfn t() -> int {\n let x = table<R2> { a | b\n  1 | 2\n  -3 | 4\n }\n 0\n}"
        agree_all(src) && exec_oracle(src) == ""
    }

    then "a table NESTED in a cell leaves the outer table's cell_mode intact" {
        let src = "fn t() -> int {\n let x = table<Row> { a | b\n  1 | table<Inner> { c\n   2\n  }\n }\n 0\n}"
        tree_agrees_everywhere(src) &&
            agree_all(src) &&
            exec_oracle(src) == ""
    }
}

